00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:10.590
of the American population yet make up more than 40%

258
00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:12.560
of the homicide victims, right?

259
00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.100
Which is unreal, which is a problem,

260
00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:17.403
which is what you would call a catastrophe.

261
00:12:18.927 --> 00:12:22.500
And I think there are parallels to the fact

262
00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:26.100
that there the fatherlessness in that community,

263
00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:29.770
in our community, it ties in to the violence.

264
00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:33.100
It ties in to when you don't have a dad around

265
00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:36.450
you go right to the gangs, you go right to those vices.

266
00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:39.927
You go right to all those things, because you do need,

267
00:12:39.927 --> 00:12:44.270
you need someone to guide you, to show you what being

268
00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:46.920
a man is like, and when we're talking

269
00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:48.304
about masculinity, you know?

270
00:12:48.304 --> 00:12:49.900
That's the question.
Right, and also

271
00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:52.380
the epigenetics of generation

272
00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:55.140
after generation repeating, you know,

273
00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:58.270
like sort of patterns that don't work and kind of

274
00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:01.910
calcifying this idea of what it means to be a man,

275
00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:04.370
because they're repeating the patterns of their father,

276
00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:07.160
even if that father was present in the household,

277
00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:10.690
like in your case, who was your model

278
00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:12.130
for how to be a man in the world?

279
00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:15.040
It's a guy who was completely locked down emotionally.

280
00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:16.060
That's right.

281
00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:19.840
But I appreciated the attempt to try to understand

282
00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:23.677
him as a guy who was also caught between the white world

283
00:13:23.677 --> 00:13:24.810
and the black world.
That's right.

284
00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:27.080
He was too advanced in the factory

285
00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:30.620
to be accepted by, you know, the guys on the floor, right?

286
00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:32.117
Who were predominantly black.
Sure. Yep.

287
00:13:32.117 --> 00:13:34.110
But he wasn't quite in the C-suite

288
00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:35.660
to be accepted by the white guys.

289
00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:38.110
So he had his own like kind of identity crisis

290
00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:40.550
that he was trying to navigate simultaneously.

291
00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:43.080
You know, a lot of people just,

292
00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:48.060
what is really, really hardcore in his walk

293
00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:50.900
was that here he was, he was trying to make it

294
00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:55.790
and he felt like he was doing the right thing,

295
00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:58.620
and then what was crazy is that he was ridiculed

296
00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:02.880
by his peers just for advancing,

297
00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:05.280
which is another problem, you know.

298
00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:10.070
And then when you advance, the people who, you know,

299
00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:11.630
kind of hold the key to your future

300
00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:14.090
wouldn't accept him completely,

301
00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:17.030
simply because he was one of the few black foreman

302
00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:18.250
in the place.

303
00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:22.160
So you had tons of white foreman all around

304
00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:24.500
and they would go on up the ladder.

305
00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:28.020
He being a black foreman kind of was stuck

306
00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:30.280
in his place 'cause he wouldn't get invited

307
00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:32.607
to the inner city, I mean the inner parties.

308
00:14:32.607 --> 00:14:33.440
Right.

309
00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:34.610
Or the inner sector, you know what I mean?

310
00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:37.350
And then the blacks who were on the floor were just like,

311
00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:38.333
oh, he's a sellout.

312
00:14:39.844 --> 00:14:42.700
And what's wild is that there's a, you know,

313
00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:45.860
there's a phrase that blacks use, you know,

314
00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:48.670
to describe what they would call, you know,

315
00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:51.230
black people who sell out, and it's a coon.

316
00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:54.690
And they look at you capitulating to,

317
00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:57.140
you know, the white world in order to be accepted

318
00:14:58.140 --> 00:14:59.640
and what you're doing, you know,

319
00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:02.223
you're doing what you have to do to make it.

320
00:15:03.832 --> 00:15:04.970
And that's a hard role.

321
00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:09.120
And he took these things personally and a good thing

322
00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:12.090
is I'm smart enough and have learned enough to know,

323
00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:16.210
to let that just, you know, for me, you know,

324
00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:18.230
these kind of insults are just like, okay,

325
00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:21.030
that has more to do with you than it does with me.

326
00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:24.500
But back in that time and that period,

327
00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:26.130
there wasn't that kind of introspection,

328
00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:26.963
you know what I mean?

329
00:15:26.963 --> 00:15:29.950
It was just like, why am I not being accepted

330
00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:30.783
by my own people?

331
00:15:30.783 --> 00:15:33.340
You know? And he was very angry.

332
00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:36.780
Like it just created this really toxic anger.

333
00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:40.100
So you have this unhealthy role model in the household.

334
00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:42.490
Meanwhile, the jobs are drying up.

335
00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:45.400
There's white flight, the gangs are coming in.

336
00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:46.233
That's right.

337
00:15:46.233 --> 00:15:47.850
So you go to school and you went to Flint Academy,

338
00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:50.660
which was like kind of the best place for you to go, right?

339
00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:54.580
But not a lot of great role models there either, right?

340
00:15:54.580 --> 00:15:55.690
A lot of temptations.

341
00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:57.340
I mean, you could have easily,

342
00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:59.710
like there's so many inflection points in your story

343
00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:02.790
and in your life, I mean, you could have easily gone down

344
00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:05.040
a dark alley with the whole gang thing.

345
00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:05.890
Yeah. Yeah.

346
00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:10.350
I mean, I have to, because now on the flip side,

347
00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:13.150
my mother was extremely religious,

348
00:16:13.150 --> 00:16:14.573
which is a whole nother aspect.

349
00:16:14.573 --> 00:16:15.406
Right.

350
00:16:15.406 --> 00:16:17.350
Yeah, that's a whole other section of the book, too.

351
00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:18.183
Right.

352
00:16:18.183 --> 00:16:20.300
I mean, we deal on all these topics

353
00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:24.150
and I kind of was right in the middle of all this stuff

354
00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:27.610
with the religion, you know, I was not allowed

355
00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:29.610
to go to secular movies,

356
00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:33.440
listen to secular music, go dance,

357
00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:35.430
go to play sports.

358
00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:39.080
Everything I ended up doing and being in my life,

359
00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:41.930
I was not allowed to do as a kid.

360
00:16:41.930 --> 00:16:44.030
But we're about the same age.

361
00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.020
So we both remember well

362
00:16:46.020 --> 00:16:48.291
that first experience of going to "Star Wars."

363
00:16:48.291 --> 00:16:49.124
Yes.

364
00:16:49.124 --> 00:16:51.270
And for you, that was like this transgressive act.

365
00:16:51.270 --> 00:16:52.710
Listen, I begged.

366
00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:54.733
My mother was not going to let us go.

367
00:16:56.210 --> 00:16:57.943
The religion that we were in,

368
00:16:59.550 --> 00:17:02.110
it was so strict.

369
00:17:02.110 --> 00:17:07.110
And so shame-based and everything was just, you know,

370
00:17:07.840 --> 00:17:10.840
the thing is you could go to hell at any moment.

371
00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:13.464
That was the through lie.

372
00:17:13.464 --> 00:17:15.010
Like anything send you to help.

373
00:17:15.010 --> 00:17:18.070
Okay, so going to a movie, oh no, you're gonna go to hell,

374
00:17:18.070 --> 00:17:19.487
you do that, you know?

375
00:17:19.487 --> 00:17:21.700
And I begged my mother and she wouldn't let me go.

376
00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:22.533
And we were just like,

377
00:17:22.533 --> 00:17:24.900
and I couldn't, 'cause we could go to school

378
00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:26.690
and you hear people talking, this is days

379
00:17:26.690 --> 00:17:28.620
when a movie was four-walled.

380
00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:31.220
So a movie would be in the theaters for a year, right?

381
00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:32.410
You know, like now it's like,

382
00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:33.860
it's out in three weeks, you know what I mean?

383
00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:35.500
But it was, would be in the movie for a year,

384
00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:37.799
two years, and still be out.

385
00:17:37.799 --> 00:17:41.390
And I would hear the kids in school just, oh, "Star Wars,"

386
00:17:41.390 --> 00:17:42.920
and they'd be talking about different parts.

387
00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:44.860
And I'm like, oh my God, I just gotta see this.

388
00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:46.503
And the anticipation,

389
00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:49.540
I just wanted to see it so bad.

390
00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:53.457
And my aunt, thank God she came through and she said,

391
00:17:53.457 --> 00:17:54.820
and my mother's name was Patricia.

392
00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:57.730
She said, "Trish, you gotta let these kids see this movie."

393
00:17:57.730 --> 00:18:00.244
You know, like, "I'm gonna take them, okay?

394
00:18:00.244 --> 00:18:02.120
Can I take them?"

395
00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:05.851
And she just broke like, "All right, go ahead."

396
00:18:05.851 --> 00:18:06.684
And I said...

397
00:18:06.684 --> 00:18:08.100
And it changed my life forever.

398
00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:11.716
I'll never forget that drive in, seeing, watching that.

399
00:18:11.716 --> 00:18:12.549
We all remember that first screen.

400
00:18:12.549 --> 00:18:13.530
That John Williams score hit,

401
00:18:13.530 --> 00:18:14.824
ah, you know what I mean?
Yeah. Yeah.

402
00:18:14.824 --> 00:18:15.760
It was it.

403
00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:17.930
So how does your mom square,

404
00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:21.530
like, what you're doing now with that mindset?

405
00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:25.340
Well, my mom passed away 2015,

406
00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:26.987
but she also left.

407
00:18:26.987 --> 00:18:28.800
She was alive long enough to-

408
00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:30.230
Yes. She left the church.

409
00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:33.250
She left that version of what the church was

410
00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:37.320
when I was probably in high school.

411
00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:42.320
And she went to a less restrictive version of Christianity.

412
00:18:43.980 --> 00:18:45.410
But I like to call it a cult.

413
00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:49.000
I mean, it was, it was very cultish in all its ways

414
00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:51.950
when you're talking about rules and all this stuff.

415
00:18:51.950 --> 00:18:55.100
So she kind of moved on from that and grew

416
00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:57.633
into loving what I was doing.

417
00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:02.330
You know, what's wild is that she kind of saw the light

418
00:19:02.330 --> 00:19:06.540
as we moved on, and I got more popular and started doing

419
00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:10.690
things she started to understand, but she, you know,

420
00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:13.000
she loved what I did at the end.

421
00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:16.470
One thing is she always, always loved entertainment,

422
00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.020
like always.

423
00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:20.240
And she had-
She had the subscriptions

424
00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:21.780
to all the magazine.
Exactly.

425
00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:23.380
People Magazine and all that kind of stuff. (laughs)

426
00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:25.790
Well, I worked hard to rectify,

427
00:19:25.790 --> 00:19:29.120
like to try to figure out what that was like.

428
00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:31.820
We can't go to the movies, but we love the movies.

429
00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:33.930
We can't do this, but we love this.

430
00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:37.087
And it was, so I used to read her People

431
00:19:37.087 --> 00:19:39.740
and Entertainment magazines, and the whole thing.

432
00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:42.030
And one thing we did together though,

433
00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:44.917
was we would sit and watch "The Carol Burnett Show."

434
00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:47.790
I'll never forget, every Saturday night.

435
00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:51.840
And it was the highlight, highlight of my life,

436
00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:55.950
because first of all, you know, I'll never forget.

437
00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:59.750
My mom was nursing a black eye that my dad gave her.

438
00:19:59.750 --> 00:20:03.070
And she had like this, it was some frozen peas

439
00:20:03.070 --> 00:20:04.780
or something on her eye.

440
00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:06.720
And we would just sit and watch Carol Burnett

441
00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:08.050
and laugh and laugh.

442
00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:09.990
And then after it was over, I would repeat

443
00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:14.050
some of the skits and she would just laugh at me doing

444
00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:17.400
some of the skits, the Tim Conway and Harvey Korman,

445
00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:18.233
and all this stuff.

446
00:20:18.233 --> 00:20:20.040
And I'll never forget that.

447
00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:24.480
I mean, even to this day, I view that as my first step

448
00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:27.410
in what I'm doing now was the attempt

449
00:20:27.410 --> 00:20:29.240
to make my mother laugh through all the pain

450
00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:30.380
she was going through.
Yeah.

451
00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:32.820
Well, there's this split in who you are

452
00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:34.190
for the better part of your life.

453
00:20:34.190 --> 00:20:38.020
Like on the one hand, you're this alpha athlete,

454
00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:39.850
guy's going in the weight room, trying to get big

455
00:20:39.850 --> 00:20:42.280
and strong to deal with what's going on at home

456
00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:43.850
and to deal with the sort

457
00:20:43.850 --> 00:20:46.680
of social stratification of school.

458
00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:49.640
But at the same time, you're a kid who's sensitive

459
00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:53.310
and likes comic books and is really good at drawing and art,

460
00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:57.200
which, you know, creates this tension in like, who am I?

461
00:20:57.200 --> 00:20:58.330
Who is Terry Crews?

462
00:20:58.330 --> 00:21:00.100
And how am I showing up in the world?

463
00:21:00.100 --> 00:21:02.490
Like these two things kind of at war with each other.

464
00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:03.540
It was at war.

465
00:21:03.540 --> 00:21:06.273
And what was wild is that,

466
00:21:07.775 --> 00:21:09.530
you know, I remember,

467
00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:11.880
you know, having to fight my way into school.

468
00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:16.694
It was, you know, the gangs, the drug dealers,

469
00:21:16.694 --> 00:21:18.910
the people that were around me

470
00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:21.733
were always looking at me suspiciously.

471
00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:26.180
They would say things like you talk white, you know.

472
00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:27.530
And I'm like, what is that?

473
00:21:28.791 --> 00:21:30.250
And what was happening is, remember,

474
00:21:30.250 --> 00:21:32.130
I was kind of sheltered, is what it was.

475
00:21:32.130 --> 00:21:33.420
I mean, I was religious.

476
00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:35.540
I was in this thing where they wouldn't allow us

477
00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:38.400
to do anything, but so I would go into my hole

478
00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:40.890
and I would draw and draw all day.

479
00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:44.250
I mean, I would draw what I wanted to see, you know,

480
00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:48.551
and it was a introspective, very nerdy world.

481
00:21:48.551 --> 00:21:49.840
I became this nerd.

482
00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:53.630
But then when I came outside, I was always challenged on it.

483
00:21:53.630 --> 00:21:55.220
Like, oh man, you, this is, you know,

484
00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:58.090
back in the day, they called you a square, you know.

485
00:21:58.090 --> 00:21:59.730
And it was like, what?

486
00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:04.360
But then athletics coded black, coded hardcore.

487
00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:06.300
So I said, all right.

488
00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:11.050
So I have to be that, you know, and I knew

489
00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:15.780
that I had to be big, strong, fast, athletic,

490
00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:17.430
and they would leave me alone.

491
00:22:17.430 --> 00:22:20.060
You know, it's like, I knew when I got some traps

492
00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:23.970
that they would think twice about they'd go, well,

493
00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:25.610
he's probably gonna do some athletic stuff,

494
00:22:25.610 --> 00:22:26.470
so they leave him alone.

495
00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:29.840
But the skinny kid, he had no choice.

496
00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:30.673
They would beat him up.

497
00:22:30.673 --> 00:22:32.500
It would be crazy. It would be violent.

498
00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:33.880
They used to beat up my brother.

499
00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:34.990
He was smaller than I was.

500
00:22:34.990 --> 00:22:36.510
He's my half-brother.

501
00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:37.670
And I remember these gang members

502
00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:42.670
threw him in the rose bush, and he was just hurt.

503
00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:45.630
We went home and he was in tears and I was just like,

504
00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:48.810
pulling him out of it, like, but they didn't mess with me.

505
00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:49.690
Yeah.

506
00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:52.900
But that coded black and it coded hardcore.

507
00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:53.733
You know what I mean?

508
00:22:53.733 --> 00:22:56.550
But it's almost like this shield, this armor

509
00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:59.440
that you built around yourself simply

510
00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:01.666
to protect that artist within you.

511
00:23:01.666 --> 00:23:02.499
Yeah.

512
00:23:02.499 --> 00:23:05.500
So he could still, you know, do his thing quietly

513
00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:09.760
without, you know, being bullied or taking the task.

