767
00:33:58.870 --> 00:34:00.490
Yeah.
But you do have,

768
00:34:00.490 --> 00:34:02.830
like, there are a couple mentors who show up.

769
00:34:02.830 --> 00:34:04.430
You have this teacher-
Yeah.

770
00:34:04.430 --> 00:34:07.070
At Flint Academy who kind of changes your life.

771
00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:07.903
He does.

772
00:34:08.820 --> 00:34:11.330
First of all, there were two people.

773
00:34:11.330 --> 00:34:13.560
One was a coach and one was a teacher.

774
00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:18.280
My teacher was my art teacher named Mr. Eickleberg.

775
00:34:18.280 --> 00:34:21.350
And he believed in me when I didn't believe even myself.

776
00:34:21.350 --> 00:34:25.790
You have to understand like for me, especially in Michigan,

777
00:34:25.790 --> 00:34:27.083
there are two Michigans.

778
00:34:27.083 --> 00:34:28.030
There's like the white Michigan.

779
00:34:28.030 --> 00:34:30.330
There's the Ann Arbor.
Yeah.

780
00:34:30.330 --> 00:34:32.100
I'm from the white Michigan.
Yeah. You know what I mean?

781
00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:34.130
There's Traverse City. It's gorgeous.

782
00:34:34.130 --> 00:34:35.800
It's pretty there by the water.

783
00:34:35.800 --> 00:34:39.730
And then there's Detroit and there's Benton Harbor.

784
00:34:39.730 --> 00:34:42.490
There's Flint. There's Muskegon.

785
00:34:42.490 --> 00:34:44.850
You know, I mean cities like that.

786
00:34:44.850 --> 00:34:49.810
And what happens is I remember people talking

787
00:34:49.810 --> 00:34:52.959
about the Cranbrook Academy of Arts, you know,

788
00:34:52.959 --> 00:34:55.339
and there were no black people there,

789
00:34:55.339 --> 00:34:56.178
you know?
Right.

790
00:34:56.178 --> 00:34:57.011
I mean, not when I was a kid, it wasn't-

791
00:34:57.011 --> 00:35:00.060
But Cranbrook is like the epic private school

792
00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:02.020
in like, kind of near Gross Point, right?

793
00:35:02.020 --> 00:35:02.853
Exactly.
Yeah.

794
00:35:02.853 --> 00:35:05.360
And it's very white, very-

795
00:35:05.360 --> 00:35:07.370
But has incredible arts program.

796
00:35:07.370 --> 00:35:09.703
Oh, incredible. No, beyond belief.

797
00:35:09.703 --> 00:35:12.340
But I said, (scoffs) I'm not going there.

798
00:35:12.340 --> 00:35:14.490
Like that's not gonna be me.

799
00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:16.983
I couldn't see a way in, right?

800
00:35:18.330 --> 00:35:20.270
But I love to draw.

801
00:35:20.270 --> 00:35:22.920
And I just was, I love doing my thing.

802
00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:24.830
And Mr. Eickleberg looked at me

803
00:35:24.830 --> 00:35:26.810
and he literally grabbed all my stuff.

804
00:35:26.810 --> 00:35:28.100
He said, "Terry," he said,

805
00:35:28.100 --> 00:35:31.350
"This is the best work I've ever seen."

806
00:35:31.350 --> 00:35:34.290
He's like, "Hey man, I'm the teacher,

807
00:35:34.290 --> 00:35:35.450
and you're better than me.

808
00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:36.283
You could teach the class."

809
00:35:36.283 --> 00:35:37.116
How old were you at the time?

810
00:35:37.116 --> 00:35:39.330
Oh, I was, shoot, in 10th grade.

811
00:35:39.330 --> 00:35:41.610
I was probably in 10th grade, 14 years old.

812
00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:43.460
And he says, "Man,"

813
00:35:43.460 --> 00:35:45.420
he said, "You have a tremendous talent."

814
00:35:45.420 --> 00:35:47.510
And I was like, "Thanks, thanks."

815
00:35:47.510 --> 00:35:51.530
And what I would do is back in the day, you know,

816
00:35:51.530 --> 00:35:53.890
when the drug dealers took over, I mean,

817
00:35:53.890 --> 00:35:55.790
the crack epidemic was hittin' hard.

818
00:35:55.790 --> 00:35:57.080
It was like through the '80s

819
00:35:57.080 --> 00:35:59.060
and then hip hop was coming along with it.

820
00:35:59.060 --> 00:36:02.070
So what I would do is I would make all these sketches

821
00:36:02.070 --> 00:36:04.700
of like Mickey Mouse as a drug dealer.

822
00:36:04.700 --> 00:36:05.910
You know, this kind of stuff.

823
00:36:05.910 --> 00:36:08.210
And they would print t-shirts.

824
00:36:08.210 --> 00:36:10.650
And that was like, I would get $25 for a t-shirt.

825
00:36:10.650 --> 00:36:12.240
My t-shirt would be all around the city

826
00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:13.850
'cause everybody grabbed it and you see him,

827
00:36:13.850 --> 00:36:16.380
he's have the cell phone and the, you know,

828
00:36:16.380 --> 00:36:19.790
all the stuff and the accoutrements, the Fila shoes,

829
00:36:19.790 --> 00:36:23.940
the Nikes, and you kind of ghettoize these characters.

830
00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:26.560
And that was one thing that I did to make money

831
00:36:26.560 --> 00:36:27.580
and kind of do that.

832
00:36:27.580 --> 00:36:29.520
And I thought that's the pinnacle.

833
00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:32.530
Like that's all the art's gonna be doing for me.

834
00:36:32.530 --> 00:36:35.073
He took my art, photographed it,

835
00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:40.870
basically put it all in to applications to all these places

836
00:36:40.870 --> 00:36:44.970
and got me a scholarship to Interlochen Arts Academy,

837
00:36:44.970 --> 00:36:48.170
which is one of the most prestigious arts academies

838
00:36:48.170 --> 00:36:49.010
in the world.

839
00:36:49.010 --> 00:36:52.150
Like Larry Page, the founder of Google went there,

840
00:36:52.150 --> 00:36:53.630
amongst everybody.

841
00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:54.520
I think Jewel went there too.

842
00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:56.820
Oh, Jewel. I've been there, it's incredible.

843
00:36:58.332 --> 00:36:59.850
It's life changing.

844
00:36:59.850 --> 00:37:02.550
And he said, "Terry," he came up to me.

845
00:37:02.550 --> 00:37:05.370
He said, "Terry," he said, "I want to give you,

846
00:37:05.370 --> 00:37:06.836
I got something to tell you."

847
00:37:06.836 --> 00:37:08.070
And I was like, "What do you mean?"

848
00:37:08.070 --> 00:37:09.660
He says, "I got an announcement.

849
00:37:09.660 --> 00:37:12.640
I got you the scholarship, one to Interlochen

850
00:37:12.640 --> 00:37:15.440
and to Western Michigan University."

851
00:37:15.440 --> 00:37:17.900
Because he had, by that time I was

852
00:37:17.900 --> 00:37:20.970
around right before my senior year.

853
00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:23.110
And he had got all that stuff for, you know.

854
00:37:23.110 --> 00:37:25.450
He did it for you.
And did it,

855
00:37:25.450 --> 00:37:27.920
and I didn't even know.
Wow.

856
00:37:27.920 --> 00:37:31.297
And he was like, "That's how good I believe you are, man.

857
00:37:31.297 --> 00:37:33.126
And you got it."

858
00:37:33.126 --> 00:37:35.970
And I just shook, like what in the world?

859
00:37:35.970 --> 00:37:37.780
And it changed my life.

860
00:37:37.780 --> 00:37:41.693
Dude, to this day, it changed my, I remember when I go,

861
00:37:41.693 --> 00:37:45.760
I went to Interlochen, out of about 500 people,

862
00:37:45.760 --> 00:37:48.210
500 kids that were there for that summer session,

863
00:37:48.210 --> 00:37:50.490
it was about six black kids, you know?

864
00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:53.640
And so, and I was part of the arts department

865
00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:54.980
and the arts program.

866
00:37:54.980 --> 00:37:57.670
And so they had this curator

867
00:37:57.670 --> 00:38:00.580
from the University of Cincinnati come and I had drawn,

868
00:38:00.580 --> 00:38:02.550
they said and you have a big on competition.

869
00:38:02.550 --> 00:38:05.740
So they were like, draw two drawings and put it

870
00:38:05.740 --> 00:38:07.580
on the board, but don't put your name on it.

871
00:38:07.580 --> 00:38:09.630
So I put it on the board and it was

872
00:38:09.630 --> 00:38:13.200
amongst this whole wall full of everyone's drawings, right?

873
00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:17.610
And they asked the curator which one was the best.

874
00:38:17.610 --> 00:38:19.140
And he went all the way around the room

875
00:38:19.140 --> 00:38:20.760
and he said that one,

876
00:38:20.760 --> 00:38:22.290
and it was mine.
Mm.

877
00:38:22.290 --> 00:38:24.590
And then he said, "So what's the next best?"

878
00:38:24.590 --> 00:38:26.990
He went all the way around the room

879
00:38:26.990 --> 00:38:29.120
and went to my other one.

880
00:38:29.120 --> 00:38:30.770
And that's when it hit me.

881
00:38:30.770 --> 00:38:32.950
The moment it hit me, and people were clapping

882
00:38:32.950 --> 00:38:35.556
and they were like, "Well, that's Terry."

883
00:38:35.556 --> 00:38:37.750
And I that's when I realized, wow, like,

884
00:38:37.750 --> 00:38:41.523
I am, I'm as good as anybody.

885
00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:43.673
'Cause I didn't believe it.

886
00:38:43.673 --> 00:38:46.510
You know, I was told, but that was the moment

887
00:38:46.510 --> 00:38:48.150
I was like, there is something here,

888
00:38:48.150 --> 00:38:50.650
like this is special.

889
00:38:50.650 --> 00:38:52.510
This is something that I can count on.

890
00:38:52.510 --> 00:38:54.260
Like, this is, this is something

891
00:38:54.260 --> 00:38:57.430
I have to invest in, you know?

892
00:38:57.430 --> 00:38:59.760
And it changed my trajectory,

893
00:38:59.760 --> 00:39:04.100
because again, I spent so much time hiding my art.

894
00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:06.810
I spent so much time trying not to be a nerd.

895
00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:09.740
You know, it was like trying not to be that guy,

896
00:39:09.740 --> 00:39:11.350
'cause I would get super excited, but oh man,

897
00:39:11.350 --> 00:39:13.810
I got these ideas, and they would look at me like,

898
00:39:13.810 --> 00:39:14.673
all right.

899
00:39:14.673 --> 00:39:15.950
Like who do you think you are?

900
00:39:15.950 --> 00:39:18.340
And I was like, okay, let me just, all right,

901
00:39:18.340 --> 00:39:19.480
don't worry about it, you know?

902
00:39:19.480 --> 00:39:20.963
Yeah.
Even family-

903
00:39:20.963 --> 00:39:23.040
Coming from like a, who do you think you are culture too.

904
00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:25.330
Like, don't get so high on your own supply.

905
00:39:25.330 --> 00:39:26.163
Totally.

906
00:39:26.163 --> 00:39:28.190
I mean, you have to understand,

907
00:39:28.190 --> 00:39:31.720
even my family members were like, who do you think you are.

908
00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:32.830
Relax.

909
00:39:32.830 --> 00:39:34.520
My father was straight up said, "Hey man,

910
00:39:34.520 --> 00:39:37.430
the white man only gonna let you go so far."

911
00:39:37.430 --> 00:39:40.020
That was his, you could put that on a t-shirt.

912
00:39:40.020 --> 00:39:41.807
He would have that on as a mantra.

913
00:39:41.807 --> 00:39:43.250
You might as well put that in a frame.

914
00:39:43.250 --> 00:39:44.731
That was his lived experience.

915
00:39:44.731 --> 00:39:45.564
That was it.

916
00:39:45.564 --> 00:39:49.190
White man's only going to let you get so far,

917
00:39:49.190 --> 00:39:51.200
so stop asking.

918
00:39:51.200 --> 00:39:54.760
Like literally stop. And I just couldn't stop.

919
00:39:54.760 --> 00:39:57.750
After I had that Interlochen experience

920
00:39:57.750 --> 00:40:01.330
and I saw what I could do, I said, "I'm not gonna stop.

921
00:40:01.330 --> 00:40:02.370
I'm not gonna stop asking."

922
00:40:02.370 --> 00:40:04.210
So you end up at Western Michigan.

923
00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:06.873
You get this scholarship, an art scholarship, to go there.

924
00:40:06.873 --> 00:40:07.706
Yep.

925
00:40:07.706 --> 00:40:09.110
The question then becomes like,

926
00:40:09.110 --> 00:40:12.150
why didn't you just go full in on the art thing?

927
00:40:12.150 --> 00:40:14.460
And instead you're like, I'm gonna play football.

928
00:40:14.460 --> 00:40:17.745
Because it was only $500. (laughs)

929
00:40:17.745 --> 00:40:20.120
Oh, okay.
Now understand.

930
00:40:20.120 --> 00:40:23.130
You could go to college back in, what, in 1987,

931
00:40:25.470 --> 00:40:27.483
you could go to college for $3,000.

932
00:40:28.740 --> 00:40:31.230
You could. And you could work and go to college.

933
00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:33.380
And for three grand, you would pay all your tuition,

934
00:40:33.380 --> 00:40:34.830
do your whole thing.

935
00:40:34.830 --> 00:40:37.490
Over the years, it turned into $100,000.

936
00:40:37.490 --> 00:40:40.780
But back then you, the concept of you working

937
00:40:40.780 --> 00:40:42.821
and going to college was pretty much normal.

938
00:40:42.821 --> 00:40:43.654
Right.

939
00:40:43.654 --> 00:40:45.450
So you still had to come up with 2,500 bucks.

940
00:40:45.450 --> 00:40:46.770
You can't work and go to college.

941
00:40:46.770 --> 00:40:49.350
Yeah, I paid $100,000 for my daughter's school.

942
00:40:49.350 --> 00:40:51.080
I was like, where she gonna get a hundred grand from?

943
00:40:51.080 --> 00:40:51.913
You know what I mean?

944
00:40:51.913 --> 00:40:53.810
It's like, she can't do that.

945
00:40:53.810 --> 00:40:56.330
So $500 went a long way,

946
00:40:56.330 --> 00:40:58.610
but it wasn't enough to pay for everything.

947
00:40:58.610 --> 00:41:03.410
And my mother went into, they went into debt.

948
00:41:03.410 --> 00:41:05.430
My father was resisting the whole time.

949
00:41:05.430 --> 00:41:06.700
Like, man, you need to do something else.

950
00:41:06.700 --> 00:41:08.780
Go to vocational school, whatever.

951
00:41:08.780 --> 00:41:10.670
I was like, look, I wanna go to college and the whole thing.

952
00:41:10.670 --> 00:41:11.600
And she was like, look,

953
00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:13.630
we only have enough money for one semester.

954
00:41:13.630 --> 00:41:15.870
I think it was actually, she said, one year.

955
00:41:15.870 --> 00:41:18.100
We'll pull together one year, that first $500

956
00:41:18.100 --> 00:41:20.040
helped that first semester.

957
00:41:20.040 --> 00:41:21.280
I can help with the other semester,

958
00:41:21.280 --> 00:41:23.080
but after that...

959
00:41:23.080 --> 00:41:26.120
And what happened is I didn't get a scholarship

960
00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:26.957
that first year.

961
00:41:26.957 --> 00:41:30.060
And I begged, I said, just gimme one more semester.

962
00:41:30.060 --> 00:41:31.660
Now you have to understand, too,

963
00:41:32.610 --> 00:41:35.860
my brother and sister were lacking,

964
00:41:35.860 --> 00:41:37.830
and I asked for everything.

965
00:41:37.830 --> 00:41:40.100
I literally was asking everybody

966
00:41:40.100 --> 00:41:42.870
to contribute and it was selfish.

967
00:41:42.870 --> 00:41:44.960
It was like, I can do this,

968
00:41:44.960 --> 00:41:47.180
but I need everybody to contribute.

969
00:41:47.180 --> 00:41:49.510
And it became with a level of resentment.

970
00:41:49.510 --> 00:41:50.638
You know what I mean?

971
00:41:50.638 --> 00:41:51.471
It was a little bit like,

972
00:41:51.471 --> 00:41:53.250
hey man, why should we put all this into just you?

973
00:41:53.250 --> 00:41:54.083
You know?

974
00:41:54.083 --> 00:41:56.150
But I was like, I'm worth it.

975
00:41:56.150 --> 00:41:57.510
I promise, I'll make it worth it.

976
00:41:57.510 --> 00:41:59.470
And they did one more semester.

977
00:41:59.470 --> 00:42:00.623
I got a scholarship.

978
00:42:01.958 --> 00:42:03.050
And that was, let me tell you,

979
00:42:03.050 --> 00:42:06.800
that's the one moment I found out and knew

980
00:42:06.800 --> 00:42:08.860
that no was negotiable, you know?

981
00:42:08.860 --> 00:42:11.420
Like as long as you just keep going,

982
00:42:11.420 --> 00:42:13.870
eventually you're going to get what you want,

983
00:42:13.870 --> 00:42:15.300
if you just don't stop.
Mm.

984
00:42:15.300 --> 00:42:18.590
And that was a big, big moment for me

985
00:42:18.590 --> 00:42:19.566
getting that scholarship.

986
00:42:19.566 --> 00:42:22.817
It was incredible, but I still had a lot of,

987
00:42:22.817 --> 00:42:24.650
and this is what's so scary,

988
00:42:24.650 --> 00:42:26.170
and I write about it in a book is just,

989
00:42:26.170 --> 00:42:29.420
I had a lot of entitlement, tons of entitlement

990
00:42:29.420 --> 00:42:32.990
because this is another thing my mother viewed religion

991
00:42:32.990 --> 00:42:35.310
like it was a magic wand.

992
00:42:35.310 --> 00:42:36.143
You know what I mean?

993
00:42:36.143 --> 00:42:40.200
It was you, you know, we name it and claim it, you know?

994
00:42:40.200 --> 00:42:43.880
In Jesus name, I command my bills paid.

995
00:42:43.880 --> 00:42:45.720
Mm-hmm.
But you do nothing.

996
00:42:45.720 --> 00:42:47.260
Not a lot of action.
Nothing.

997
00:42:47.260 --> 00:42:49.870
Just faith.
To get those bills paid.

998
00:42:49.870 --> 00:42:53.610
Like you, I demand, in Jesus name,

999
00:42:53.610 --> 00:42:58.610
I'm gonna lose 100 pounds, and die of obesity.

1000
00:43:00.160 --> 00:43:02.620
But they wouldn't, the lifestyle would never change.

1001
00:43:02.620 --> 00:43:03.453
Nothing would ever.

1002
00:43:03.453 --> 00:43:06.940
So, you know, Jesus became a fairy godmother

1003
00:43:07.810 --> 00:43:12.317
and I saw this and I was like, but why don't...

1004
00:43:12.317 --> 00:43:15.693
And I realized that I needed to combine,

1005
00:43:16.685 --> 00:43:19.950
I realized later, that I needed to combine

1006
00:43:19.950 --> 00:43:21.740
the talk with the action.

1007
00:43:21.740 --> 00:43:24.750
But up until that point, I was still a little bit of that

1008
00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:28.514
same like name it, claim it like, hey, Jesus got me this.

1009
00:43:28.514 --> 00:43:30.410
And so I was super entitled.

1010
00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:32.260
Like I didn't really want to work.

1011
00:43:32.260 --> 00:43:33.093
You know what I mean?

1012
00:43:33.093 --> 00:43:34.163
It was like everything,

1013
00:43:35.030 --> 00:43:37.650
it was out of my control, you know what I mean?

1014
00:43:37.650 --> 00:43:40.720
And I basically, you know,

1015
00:43:40.720 --> 00:43:44.010
was expecting a handout in a lot of ways.

1016
00:43:44.010 --> 00:43:47.140
And my whole attitude was just a mess, man.

1017
00:43:47.140 --> 00:43:51.150
Like to the point where I wouldn't work, you know,

1018
00:43:51.150 --> 00:43:55.470
after I got the scholarship, I felt like somebody owed me.

1019
00:43:55.470 --> 00:43:57.710
I had a lot of resentment to the school,

1020
00:43:57.710 --> 00:43:59.500
even while I was playing.
Mm.

1021
00:43:59.500 --> 00:44:03.450
Because I was like, you know, about time, you know?

1022
00:44:03.450 --> 00:44:06.130
And it was, it's weird how these little,

1023
00:44:06.130 --> 00:44:07.860
what you would call victories could actually

1024
00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:11.710
be the thing that keep you from true success.

1025
00:44:11.710 --> 00:44:13.000
You know what I mean?
Yeah. It's interesting.

1026
00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:15.420
I mean, from an outsider looking in,

1027
00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:18.400
it looks like you get this scholarship, it's not enough,

1028
00:44:18.400 --> 00:44:20.140
so you had the wherewithal to say,

1029
00:44:20.140 --> 00:44:22.480
I'm gonna walk onto the football team

1030
00:44:22.480 --> 00:44:24.260
and turn this into a scholarship

1031
00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:26.320
and then turn this into an NFL career.

1032
00:44:26.320 --> 00:44:28.830
That requires a lot of action, right?

1033
00:44:28.830 --> 00:44:29.663
Right. Right.

1034
00:44:29.663 --> 00:44:31.550
And a lot of self-belief.
Right.

1035
00:44:31.550 --> 00:44:34.030
But that doesn't get done unless you're committed,

1036
00:44:34.030 --> 00:44:35.830
because the odds are stacked against you.

1037
00:44:35.830 --> 00:44:36.763
Yep. Yep.

1038
00:44:37.980 --> 00:44:42.583
I have to say, even with the NFL,

1039
00:44:44.120 --> 00:44:45.850
this is another thing about the NFL,

1040
00:44:45.850 --> 00:44:47.540
which was really eye opening.

1041
00:44:47.540 --> 00:44:50.450
You know, you think it's discipline,

1042
00:44:50.450 --> 00:44:51.283
but it's not.

1043
00:44:52.360 --> 00:44:53.470
You know, someone's telling you

1044
00:44:53.470 --> 00:44:55.060
when to wake up or telling you

1045
00:44:55.060 --> 00:44:57.450
when to eat, telling you when to work out,

1046
00:44:57.450 --> 00:44:58.703
telling you when to practice and telling you

1047
00:44:58.703 --> 00:45:00.083
when to go to sleep.

1048
00:45:01.270 --> 00:45:04.380
This is why so many athletes have problems

1049
00:45:04.380 --> 00:45:05.480
when their career is over.

1050
00:45:05.480 --> 00:45:07.660
'Cause there's no one to tell you this stuff anymore.

1051
00:45:07.660 --> 00:45:09.320
It's not self-discipline.

1052
00:45:09.320 --> 00:45:11.700
It's the discipline that someone else

1053
00:45:11.700 --> 00:45:13.630
is telling you to get everything done,

1054
00:45:13.630 --> 00:45:16.260
but you're not going to succeed unless

1055
00:45:16.260 --> 00:45:18.430
you know how to do this yourself.

1056
00:45:18.430 --> 00:45:20.187
It's just not gonna happen.
Yeah.

1057
00:45:20.187 --> 00:45:23.770
And I was left without knowing any of that.

1058
00:45:23.770 --> 00:45:27.920
I mean, your story of transcending that into an even,

1059
00:45:27.920 --> 00:45:31.550
you know, bigger career is so the exception,

1060
00:45:31.550 --> 00:45:34.640
but you know, most guys, we think of NFL players,

1061
00:45:34.640 --> 00:45:37.570
they're ballers, they're making cash, they're living large.

1062
00:45:37.570 --> 00:45:39.810
Most guys have your experience,

1063
00:45:39.810 --> 00:45:42.550
which is every week they're vying for their job.

1064
00:45:42.550 --> 00:45:43.480
You're getting traded.

1065
00:45:43.480 --> 00:45:46.450
You're on five or six teams for maybe two years.

1066
00:45:46.450 --> 00:45:48.760
And then you call it a day and it's over.

1067
00:45:48.760 --> 00:45:49.960
And you're broke.
Yeah.

1068
00:45:49.960 --> 00:45:54.140
Most NFL players go broke probably within five years

1069
00:45:54.140 --> 00:45:55.777
of having their NFL career.

1070
00:45:55.777 --> 00:45:58.927
And how are you ever gonna recapture that,

1071
00:45:58.927 --> 00:46:01.210
you know, kind of excitement

1072
00:46:01.210 --> 00:46:02.890
about how you're living your life?

1073
00:46:02.890 --> 00:46:05.360
Nothing's gonna replace that.
You see, that's the mistake.

1074
00:46:05.360 --> 00:46:09.190
See, that's what's so crazy, I think is that,

1075
00:46:09.190 --> 00:46:12.610
can you imagine being 30 years old and the best thing

1076
00:46:12.610 --> 00:46:16.190
that was ever gonna happen to you has already happened?

1077
00:46:16.190 --> 00:46:17.170
Mm.

1078
00:46:17.170 --> 00:46:19.460
That's nothing but depression right ahead.

1079
00:46:19.460 --> 00:46:20.940
That's nothing but depression in the future.

1080
00:46:20.940 --> 00:46:23.520
There's no nothing to look forward to.

1081
00:46:23.520 --> 00:46:26.433
And then not only that, you fucked it up.

1082
00:46:27.480 --> 00:46:28.660
Mm-hmm.
And people do not hesitate.

1083
00:46:28.660 --> 00:46:31.180
And your whole life for 15 years before that

1084
00:46:31.180 --> 00:46:32.840
was all about getting to that place.

1085
00:46:32.840 --> 00:46:35.400
So there wasn't a lot of bandwidth for even thinking

1086
00:46:35.400 --> 00:46:36.810
about anything outside of that.

1087
00:46:36.810 --> 00:46:37.643
The fact that you had

1088
00:46:37.643 --> 00:46:39.323
this gift as an artist and something

1089
00:46:39.323 --> 00:46:42.240
that you were cultivating all along the way and using

1090
00:46:42.240 --> 00:46:46.920
as a means of survival is unique and unusual.

1091
00:46:46.920 --> 00:46:47.836
Yeah.

1092
00:46:47.836 --> 00:46:50.210
I would go back in the locker rooms once I got cut

1093
00:46:50.210 --> 00:46:51.950
and I would ask the star players

1094
00:46:51.950 --> 00:46:53.402
if they wanted their portraits painted.

1095
00:46:53.402 --> 00:46:54.235
(laughs) Yeah. Yeah.

1096
00:46:54.235 --> 00:46:56.810
And I would get like five grand a painting

1097
00:46:56.810 --> 00:46:59.810
and I would do three paintings and I would get 15 grand.

1098
00:46:59.810 --> 00:47:02.100
And that would get me through the off-season.

1099
00:47:02.100 --> 00:47:03.893
You know, we were poor, man.

1100
00:47:04.960 --> 00:47:08.010
But when told, let me, the biggest story, I tell

1101
00:47:08.010 --> 00:47:10.273
that was my super wake up call.

1102
00:47:11.610 --> 00:47:14.750
You know, my best friend to this day is a guy

1103
00:47:14.750 --> 00:47:16.970
named Ken Harvey who used to play with the Redskins

1104
00:47:16.970 --> 00:47:18.270
Right.
I was his backup.

1105
00:47:18.270 --> 00:47:21.950
And when I moved to LA, he helped us move to LA.

1106
00:47:21.950 --> 00:47:23.599
He gave us a loan and he actually,

1107
00:47:23.599 --> 00:47:25.110
I wouldn't even call it a loan.

1108
00:47:25.110 --> 00:47:25.943
It was a gift.

1109
00:47:25.943 --> 00:47:27.220
He was like, just go on out there.

1110
00:47:27.220 --> 00:47:30.580
I believe in your talent, he knew I was a good artist.

1111
00:47:30.580 --> 00:47:33.040
And he was like going out there and make your way.

1112
00:47:33.040 --> 00:47:34.530
And I didn't come out to LA to act.

1113
00:47:34.530 --> 00:47:36.530
I was actually trying to get behind the scenes.

1114
00:47:36.530 --> 00:47:37.930
I saw myself as a creator.

1115
00:47:37.930 --> 00:47:39.190
I mean, I was trying to do

1116
00:47:39.190 --> 00:47:41.880
that whole industrial light magic thing.

1117
00:47:41.880 --> 00:47:43.720
I was special effects artist.

1118
00:47:43.720 --> 00:47:46.870
I had my portfolio in that Disney, at Dreamworks-

1119
00:47:46.870 --> 00:47:49.982
Yeah, you show 'em the drug dealer Mickey Mouse?

1120
00:47:49.982 --> 00:47:51.791
Yeah. (laughs) Not that one.

1121
00:47:51.791 --> 00:47:53.375
Might get a cease and desist letter

1122
00:47:53.375 --> 00:47:54.208
for that one.
Yeah.

1123
00:47:54.208 --> 00:47:55.423
I definitely didn't show 'em that one,

1124
00:47:55.423 --> 00:47:56.855
but it was in the portfolio.

1125
00:47:56.855 --> 00:47:58.690
(laughs) Uh-huh.
Okay.

1126
00:47:58.690 --> 00:48:02.420
But what was crazy is that I thought, man,

1127
00:48:02.420 --> 00:48:03.760
this is gonna be my way.

1128
00:48:03.760 --> 00:48:06.640
I can actually draw my way into Hollywood.

1129
00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:08.720
And then "Toy Story" came out

1130
00:48:08.720 --> 00:48:11.120
and all hand-drawn animation was over.

1131
00:48:11.120 --> 00:48:12.420
Mm.
Overnight.

1132
00:48:12.420 --> 00:48:15.440
I remember submitting my artwork for "Prince of Egypt."

1133
00:48:15.440 --> 00:48:18.067
That was one of the last hand-drawn animation movies done,

1134
00:48:18.067 --> 00:48:20.960
and then when toy story hit, bam, it was over.

1135
00:48:20.960 --> 00:48:25.770
So, but I had this pride and entitlement still,

1136
00:48:25.770 --> 00:48:27.270
and I was going through depression

1137
00:48:27.270 --> 00:48:28.580
because football was over.

1138
00:48:28.580 --> 00:48:30.227
It didn't end the way I wanted it to end,

1139
00:48:30.227 --> 00:48:32.680
and you always have these lofty goals, and I was done.

1140
00:48:32.680 --> 00:48:36.393
We were in this really, you know, terrible duplex,

1141
00:48:37.350 --> 00:48:38.660
just trying to make ends meet.

1142
00:48:38.660 --> 00:48:39.950
I had no money. I was broke.

1143
00:48:39.950 --> 00:48:41.010
My wife, and this is the thing.

1144
00:48:41.010 --> 00:48:44.538
My wife, the whole time was like, you need a job.

1145
00:48:44.538 --> 00:48:45.836
Mm-hmm.
And I was like, come on.

1146
00:48:45.836 --> 00:48:48.200
You know, I'm pro football player.

1147
00:48:48.200 --> 00:48:49.180
It's gonna look crazy.

1148
00:48:49.180 --> 00:48:52.380
I'm not gonna be just doing anything, right?

1149
00:48:52.380 --> 00:48:55.270
And so I kept asking my friend for loans,

1150
00:48:55.270 --> 00:48:57.930
and Ken was giving me a loan and what was happening,

1151
00:48:57.930 --> 00:49:00.040
he would give me, he was a gold bug

1152
00:49:00.040 --> 00:49:02.460
and he would have all this gold bullion.

1153
00:49:02.460 --> 00:49:06.090
Some financial advisor had told him to put money in gold.

1154
00:49:06.090 --> 00:49:07.210
So it wouldn't, you know,

1155
00:49:07.210 --> 00:49:09.640
back then that was a big thing.

1156
00:49:09.640 --> 00:49:10.540
A lot of players were doing it.

1157
00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:13.286
Got like a stash of bars?
Yeah. It was coins.

1158
00:49:13.286 --> 00:49:14.380
(laughs) Uh-huh.

1159
00:49:14.380 --> 00:49:16.820
He would have these rolls of gold coins,

1160
00:49:16.820 --> 00:49:18.470
and they would be stashed around the house.

1161
00:49:18.470 --> 00:49:19.303
You know what I mean?

1162
00:49:19.303 --> 00:49:20.710
And that's the new...

1163
00:49:20.710 --> 00:49:22.640
Now, this cryptocurrency is the big deal,

1164
00:49:22.640 --> 00:49:24.980
but back then it was gold bullion.

1165
00:49:24.980 --> 00:49:26.050
And people were always, you know,

1166
00:49:26.050 --> 00:49:27.950
they were advising people to have some of your money

1167
00:49:27.950 --> 00:49:30.070
in gold and some of your money in this.

1168
00:49:30.070 --> 00:49:33.720
He would give me like a stack of coins and he would say,

1169
00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:37.600
Hey man, whatever the price of gold is that day, turn it in.

1170
00:49:37.600 --> 00:49:39.230
And each coin was like 300 bucks.

1171
00:49:39.230 --> 00:49:41.710
So he'd send me like five to six coins.

1172
00:49:41.710 --> 00:49:43.480
And that would get me through the month,

1173
00:49:43.480 --> 00:49:46.810
but come six, seven, eight months down,

1174
00:49:46.810 --> 00:49:50.050
I'm still asking for loans, right?

1175
00:49:50.050 --> 00:49:51.510
And we are going broke, man.

1176
00:49:51.510 --> 00:49:53.040
And I'm mean, when I say broke,

1177
00:49:53.040 --> 00:49:57.170
we were digging into couches for just money for milk.

1178
00:49:57.170 --> 00:49:58.777
And it was like, and my wife was like,

1179
00:49:58.777 --> 00:50:01.685
"Why don't you get a job, man?"

1180
00:50:01.685 --> 00:50:03.130
And I was like, "Yeah, but it's gonna work."

1181
00:50:03.130 --> 00:50:05.690
And 'cause I still had this fantasy

1182
00:50:05.690 --> 00:50:07.710
of something's gonna come save me.

1183
00:50:07.710 --> 00:50:09.510
Sure.
Like a Mr. Eickleberg

1184
00:50:09.510 --> 00:50:10.400
with a scholarship.
Right.

1185
00:50:10.400 --> 00:50:13.677
Or this and this or again, Jesus is gonna save us.

1186
00:50:13.677 --> 00:50:14.510
You know what I mean?
Don't you know

1187
00:50:14.510 --> 00:50:15.640
who I think I am?

1188
00:50:15.640 --> 00:50:18.190
And without me working at all,

1189
00:50:18.190 --> 00:50:20.100
I was using my mother's technique,

1190
00:50:20.100 --> 00:50:21.390
right?
Mm-hmm. Claim it.

1191
00:50:21.390 --> 00:50:24.267
And one day he said, "Man, no more loans."

1192
00:50:25.830 --> 00:50:26.663
Oh, okay. Okay.

1193
00:50:27.850 --> 00:50:29.560
Now, when your boys gets all high like that,

1194
00:50:29.560 --> 00:50:31.920
you know you're doing something wrong. (laughs)

1195
00:50:31.920 --> 00:50:34.077
I was like, "Oh, okay. Okay"

1196
00:50:34.077 --> 00:50:36.340
'Cause you know, you overstepped, you know?

1197
00:50:36.340 --> 00:50:37.173
Oh my God.

1198
00:50:37.173 --> 00:50:41.050
It was the first moment of like, he just pushed back,

1199
00:50:41.050 --> 00:50:43.890
and I hung up and I was like,

1200
00:50:43.890 --> 00:50:46.840
"I don't know who the fuck is he. Fuck him."

1201
00:50:46.840 --> 00:50:50.690
My whole attitude was like, he's supposed to be a friend.

1202
00:50:50.690 --> 00:50:52.450
And he's supposed to help me out.

1203
00:50:52.450 --> 00:50:54.080
And I'm saying, he knows I'm out here.

1204
00:50:54.080 --> 00:50:55.210
He knows I'm struggling.

1205
00:50:55.210 --> 00:50:56.923
What the hell, man? Fuck you.

1206
00:50:58.230 --> 00:51:00.170
And then this little voice came to me like,

1207
00:51:00.170 --> 00:51:04.480
Hey man, why are you mad at the only person

1208
00:51:04.480 --> 00:51:06.980
who helped you in the first place?

1209
00:51:06.980 --> 00:51:08.130
Yeah.
I went...

1210
00:51:08.130 --> 00:51:09.650
My family didn't give me any money.

1211
00:51:09.650 --> 00:51:10.550
Nobody else gave me.

1212
00:51:10.550 --> 00:51:13.230
He's the one who actually was giving me money.

1213
00:51:13.230 --> 00:51:15.783
I wasn't angry at anybody else who told me no.

1214
00:51:16.630 --> 00:51:19.870
I was only angry at the person who actually hooked me up.

1215
00:51:19.870 --> 00:51:22.450
Right, which is related to resenting Western Michigan

1216
00:51:22.450 --> 00:51:24.412
for giving you a scholarship.
You see what I mean?

1217
00:51:24.412 --> 00:51:26.330
It's that weird thing.
Same thing.

1218
00:51:26.330 --> 00:51:29.730
Imagine if you just gave $10 to some person every day

1219
00:51:29.730 --> 00:51:30.970
and all of a sudden you go, that's it.

1220
00:51:30.970 --> 00:51:34.170
They would go, "What the? Where's my 10 bucks?"

1221
00:51:34.170 --> 00:51:35.710
That's where I was at.

1222
00:51:35.710 --> 00:51:38.773
And I was like, I didn't deserve that.

1223
00:51:38.773 --> 00:51:40.170
I never earned it.

1224
00:51:40.170 --> 00:51:41.517
It was something that was a gift.

1225
00:51:41.517 --> 00:51:43.263
And he was like, no more.

1226
00:51:44.682 --> 00:51:48.450
And it hit me, man. I said, "I'm wrong, man."

1227
00:51:48.450 --> 00:51:51.270
But remember, remember my wife was telling me

1228
00:51:51.270 --> 00:51:55.287
this the whole time, but because I was a man

1229
00:51:55.287 --> 00:51:59.490
and because I viewed myself as more valuable than she was,

1230
00:51:59.490 --> 00:52:02.160
all her advice was secondary.

1231
00:52:02.160 --> 00:52:04.250
It was as if my kids were telling me something.

1232
00:52:04.250 --> 00:52:05.083
Yeah.

1233
00:52:05.083 --> 00:52:06.930
And 'cause I came at it as this attitude

1234
00:52:06.930 --> 00:52:11.040
of what masculinity is you you're supposed to already know.

1235
00:52:11.040 --> 00:52:13.740
So, you know, what your wife is saying

1236
00:52:13.740 --> 00:52:15.700
is mah-mah-mah-mah.

1237
00:52:15.700 --> 00:52:17.730
She was telling the truth the whole time.

1238
00:52:17.730 --> 00:52:18.660
But it's so crazy.

1239
00:52:18.660 --> 00:52:22.440
I would listen when Ken said it, it hit me.

1240
00:52:22.440 --> 00:52:25.950
Another man told me, I was like, oh damn.

1241
00:52:25.950 --> 00:52:30.250
And so the next day I got up like 4:30 in the morning,

1242
00:52:30.250 --> 00:52:32.150
went to this place called Labor Ready.

1243
00:52:33.257 --> 00:52:34.090
I mean, I'll never forget it.

1244
00:52:34.090 --> 00:52:37.800
It was in North Hollywood, five in the morning.

1245
00:52:37.800 --> 00:52:42.800
I show up with drug addicts, halfway house guys,

1246
00:52:43.030 --> 00:52:45.220
you know, straight out of prison,

1247
00:52:45.220 --> 00:52:48.330
trying to make it, whole thing, homeless.

1248
00:52:48.330 --> 00:52:51.050
And I show up and what they do at Labor Ready

1249
00:52:51.050 --> 00:52:52.790
is give you a job for the day

1250
00:52:52.790 --> 00:52:54.690
and you get paid at the end of the day.

1251
00:52:54.690 --> 00:52:57.683
And they sent me to this place called Whitecap.

1252
00:52:58.570 --> 00:53:00.260
And it was somewhere, I forget,

1253
00:53:00.260 --> 00:53:04.190
I think it was somewhere in the valley, right?

1254
00:53:04.190 --> 00:53:05.820
Some valley somewhere.

1255
00:53:05.820 --> 00:53:09.540
And I would go in there and hand me a broom.

1256
00:53:09.540 --> 00:53:10.660
And let me tell you some man,

1257
00:53:10.660 --> 00:53:12.810
I thought I was gonna pass out.

1258
00:53:12.810 --> 00:53:15.830
I said, if I start, like man,

1259
00:53:15.830 --> 00:53:16.990
I was in the NFL.
(Rich laughs)

1260
00:53:16.990 --> 00:53:21.580
Now I got this broom in my hand in a factory.

1261
00:53:21.580 --> 00:53:23.423
And I'm like, oh, oh.

1262
00:53:23.423 --> 00:53:25.890
I thought I was just going to fall to the ground.

1263
00:53:25.890 --> 00:53:27.360
All of a sudden I took that thing,

1264
00:53:27.360 --> 00:53:29.780
and I'm in tears, just welling up.

1265
00:53:29.780 --> 00:53:32.500
Like, this might be my life.

1266
00:53:32.500 --> 00:53:34.590
This might be where I'm at.

1267
00:53:34.590 --> 00:53:36.010
How did I fall so low?

1268
00:53:36.010 --> 00:53:39.590
How did I get from the NFL and all the promise

1269
00:53:39.590 --> 00:53:42.330
of the artwork and the neh-neh-neh and the scholarship,

1270
00:53:42.330 --> 00:53:47.223
and then I'm sweeping the damn floor in a factory.

1271
00:53:48.700 --> 00:53:53.370
But this is the craziness about it, and was so wow.

1272
00:53:53.370 --> 00:53:58.370
I started sweeping and all of a sudden I felt an energy

1273
00:54:00.240 --> 00:54:02.237
and I remember people were like, man,

1274
00:54:02.237 --> 00:54:04.178
"Hey man, you look a little familiar."

1275
00:54:04.178 --> 00:54:05.157
I was like, "Man, you don't know me."

1276
00:54:05.157 --> 00:54:07.770
And I pulled my hat down a little further

1277
00:54:07.770 --> 00:54:11.080
and I kept sweeping, but I got new energy

1278
00:54:11.080 --> 00:54:14.540
because I was actually for the first time

1279
00:54:14.540 --> 00:54:16.883
doing something about my situation.

1280
00:54:17.740 --> 00:54:19.610
Whereas before I was just sit and wait.

1281
00:54:19.610 --> 00:54:21.640
Yeah.
And I was like, oh my God.

1282
00:54:21.640 --> 00:54:23.460
And so I was like, let me get in the corners

1283
00:54:23.460 --> 00:54:25.210
and let me do, let me do this job.

1284
00:54:25.210 --> 00:54:28.830
'Cause another thing is the resisting work

1285
00:54:28.830 --> 00:54:32.530
is the hard part, but embracing work,

1286
00:54:32.530 --> 00:54:34.640
the day goes by like that.

1287
00:54:34.640 --> 00:54:35.473
And-
Well-

1288
00:54:35.473 --> 00:54:36.630
You know what I mean?
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1289
00:54:36.630 --> 00:54:37.463
No, I got it.

1290
00:54:37.463 --> 00:54:40.470
I mean, it's interesting that you were able to like

1291
00:54:40.470 --> 00:54:41.760
do that math quickly.

1292
00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:43.700
Like generally takes a lot longer

1293
00:54:43.700 --> 00:54:46.540
before you kind of are lured into a place

1294
00:54:46.540 --> 00:54:48.870
of surrender to it, but that acceptance,

1295
00:54:48.870 --> 00:54:51.190
like I'm gonna be a worker among workers

1296
00:54:51.190 --> 00:54:55.260
is an act of humbling yourself.

1297
00:54:55.260 --> 00:54:57.603
But that's the place where you can begin to grow.

1298
00:54:57.603 --> 00:55:00.000
I mean, this is like a big, you know, you're sober now.

1299
00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:04.390
Like you understand like that piece, that acceptance,

1300
00:55:04.390 --> 00:55:08.260
and that humility is so important to growth, right?

1301
00:55:08.260 --> 00:55:11.654
Getting over your own, you know, self-aggrandizement,

1302
00:55:11.654 --> 00:55:13.760
idea of who you think you should be

1303
00:55:13.760 --> 00:55:16.310
and how people are supposed to interact with you

1304
00:55:16.310 --> 00:55:19.340
is a really beautiful thing, right?

1305
00:55:19.340 --> 00:55:20.520
And as you're telling that story,

1306
00:55:20.520 --> 00:55:22.990
I'm thinking about your dad polishing his shoes.

1307
00:55:22.990 --> 00:55:24.690
Like you have so little agency,

1308
00:55:24.690 --> 00:55:27.483
it's like the Serenity Prayer, right?

1309
00:55:27.483 --> 00:55:28.316
Yes.

1310
00:55:28.316 --> 00:55:29.149
Like there are so few things

1311
00:55:29.149 --> 00:55:30.300
that you have control over.

1312
00:55:30.300 --> 00:55:31.779
But you know what I have control over?

1313
00:55:31.779 --> 00:55:33.820
I have control over how I conduct myself

1314
00:55:33.820 --> 00:55:35.350
with this job today.
Yes.

1315
00:55:35.350 --> 00:55:38.200
And I can do it with a sense of gratitude

1316
00:55:38.200 --> 00:55:39.200
and pride.
That's right.

1317
00:55:39.200 --> 00:55:40.680
And that's gonna make it worth it.

1318
00:55:40.680 --> 00:55:41.830
Oh man.

1319
00:55:41.830 --> 00:55:46.100
There is a poem that I heard that I just love.

1320
00:55:46.100 --> 00:55:49.190
It says, and I can't pronounce the name

1321
00:55:49.190 --> 00:55:50.930
of the guy who wrote it.

1322
00:55:50.930 --> 00:55:55.087
I'll massacre it, but it goes, it says,

1323
00:55:55.087 --> 00:55:58.120
"I slept and dreamt of joy.

1324
00:55:58.120 --> 00:56:01.870
I awoke and realized

1325
00:56:01.870 --> 00:56:03.563
life was service.

1326
00:56:05.370 --> 00:56:08.990
I worked and then behold,

1327
00:56:08.990 --> 00:56:10.880
service was joy."

1328
00:56:10.880 --> 00:56:13.470
And that's whoa.

1329
00:56:13.470 --> 00:56:15.830
Whoa. That's it.

1330
00:56:15.830 --> 00:56:18.430
That's beautiful.
It's so beautiful.

1331
00:56:18.430 --> 00:56:21.680
And I went, that's what was happening.

1332
00:56:21.680 --> 00:56:24.300
And I did that eight hours.

1333
00:56:24.300 --> 00:56:26.170
I went back, got my check.

1334
00:56:26.170 --> 00:56:27.830
It was like $64.

1335
00:56:27.830 --> 00:56:29.160
They took out the taxes.

1336
00:56:29.160 --> 00:56:30.970
I had $48.

1337
00:56:30.970 --> 00:56:33.690
I put $20 in the gas tank.

1338
00:56:33.690 --> 00:56:36.010
I gave $20 to my wife for groceries

1339
00:56:36.010 --> 00:56:38.560
and I had $8 in my hand.

1340
00:56:38.560 --> 00:56:41.260
And I said, I will never be broke again,

1341
00:56:41.260 --> 00:56:43.750
because you know, you said it takes a while,

1342
00:56:43.750 --> 00:56:45.250
but it happened.

1343
00:56:45.250 --> 00:56:47.800
It kind of cracked that egg when I walked in there.

1344
00:56:48.723 --> 00:56:51.550
The fact that I actually got in the car that morning

1345
00:56:51.550 --> 00:56:55.153
and went in was you it's like, that's the egg cracking.

1346
00:56:56.399 --> 00:56:58.400
The sweeping was when it started to sizzle.

1347
00:56:58.400 --> 00:56:59.233
You know what I mean?

1348
00:56:59.233 --> 00:57:03.560
But I had, 'cause I would spend months not moving,

1349
00:57:03.560 --> 00:57:08.120
but that, I mean, I broke through that thing

1350
00:57:08.120 --> 00:57:10.630
'cause I thought everyone was gonna look at me

1351
00:57:10.630 --> 00:57:14.269
and everyone's gonna judge me and nobody cared.

1352
00:57:14.269 --> 00:57:15.945
No. 'Cause they're thinking about themselves. (laughs)

1353
00:57:15.945 --> 00:57:18.352
Exactly. No one is thinking about you.

1354
00:57:18.352 --> 00:57:20.170
You know what I mean? No one even knew me.

1355
00:57:20.170 --> 00:57:24.590
And that was this obstacle, this huge like wall

1356
00:57:24.590 --> 00:57:28.390
that I felt like I had to climb and I realized nobody gives

1357
00:57:28.390 --> 00:57:30.320
a damn about where I'm at.
Right.

1358
00:57:30.320 --> 00:57:31.840
So how long did you do that for?

1359
00:57:31.840 --> 00:57:34.083
Oh man, probably a a week.

1360
00:57:34.950 --> 00:57:35.830
A week.

1361
00:57:35.830 --> 00:57:39.280
And then, well, I went and applied for another

1362
00:57:39.280 --> 00:57:41.590
'cause once I broke that I went and applied

1363
00:57:41.590 --> 00:57:44.560
at another place called Western Staff Services.

1364
00:57:44.560 --> 00:57:46.900
And then I got a job being a,

1365
00:57:46.900 --> 00:57:49.570
I was filing all the papers that fell over

1366
00:57:49.570 --> 00:57:51.280
in the Northridge Earthquake

1367
00:57:51.280 --> 00:57:52.600
at the Veteran's Administration.

1368
00:57:52.600 --> 00:57:54.045
I was like this better than sweeping.

1369
00:57:54.045 --> 00:57:54.878
Right.

1370
00:57:54.878 --> 00:57:56.720
And so, but I was a filer.

1371
00:57:56.720 --> 00:57:59.370
And so I did that for a year.

1372
00:57:59.370 --> 00:58:02.970
But I didn't have to do the hard labor stuff.

1373
00:58:02.970 --> 00:58:05.660
But let me tell you something, man.

1374
00:58:05.660 --> 00:58:07.710
As I started to go and I started to,

1375
00:58:07.710 --> 00:58:11.063
I said, okay, we're here, this is my life now.

1376
00:58:11.960 --> 00:58:14.120
And I started to grow. I started to grow.

1377
00:58:14.120 --> 00:58:15.930
Now, mind you, there was still a lot of things

1378
00:58:15.930 --> 00:58:17.350
I didn't get right.

1379
00:58:17.350 --> 00:58:21.860
But I started to understand the entitlement piece.

1380
00:58:21.860 --> 00:58:24.740
It was like, you are not owed anything.

1381
00:58:24.740 --> 00:58:27.300
And I started to say, you have to work for this.

1382
00:58:27.300 --> 00:58:29.723
You know, this is not a, you gotta understand

1383
00:58:29.723 --> 00:58:31.750
that that religious mindset was actually

1384
00:58:31.750 --> 00:58:33.220
like a lottery mentality.

1385
00:58:33.220 --> 00:58:34.053
You know what I mean?

1386
00:58:34.053 --> 00:58:36.730
We just keep playing God, we keep rolling on God.

1387
00:58:36.730 --> 00:58:38.300
It's going to hit for us one day.

1388
00:58:38.300 --> 00:58:39.133
You know what I mean?

1389
00:58:39.133 --> 00:58:40.760
And you're left with nothing,

1390
00:58:40.760 --> 00:58:42.967
but I realized I can do some, like you said,

1391
00:58:42.967 --> 00:58:44.470
it was the Serenity Prayer,

1392
00:58:44.470 --> 00:58:47.300
the things that I can control, control it.

1393
00:58:47.300 --> 00:58:48.980
Mm-hmm.
You know what I mean?

1394
00:58:48.980 --> 00:58:51.050
But for a long, long time I felt

1395
00:58:51.050 --> 00:58:54.740
I couldn't even control what happened to me like myself.

1396
00:58:54.740 --> 00:58:58.230
I couldn't like if the wind went right, I was going right.

1397
00:58:58.230 --> 00:59:01.830
And the wind went that way, I would succumb to that,

1398
00:59:01.830 --> 00:59:03.970
and I realized I could walk straight.

1399
00:59:03.970 --> 00:59:07.967
I could really, really handle my own business and myself.

1400
00:59:07.967 --> 00:59:12.160
And then I started doing security for the movie sets.

1401
00:59:12.160 --> 00:59:14.683
And let me tell you something, I loved that gig.

1402
00:59:15.800 --> 00:59:19.150
It's wild when I look back, the first movie I ever did,

1403
00:59:19.150 --> 00:59:20.587
as security forward was a movie called

1404
00:59:20.587 --> 00:59:23.340
"Man on the Moon" with Jim Carey, Danny DeVito.

1405
00:59:23.340 --> 00:59:24.260
Yeah, sure.

1406
00:59:24.260 --> 00:59:26.030
Milos Forman was the director.

1407
00:59:26.030 --> 00:59:27.560
Yeah.
And I was standing in,

1408
00:59:27.560 --> 00:59:30.670
I'll never forget in my cast security shirt

1409
00:59:30.670 --> 00:59:32.600
and I would iron my shirts and put batteries

1410
00:59:32.600 --> 00:59:35.740
in my flashlight and I would work out and make sure

1411
00:59:35.740 --> 00:59:36.631
that I looked great.

1412
00:59:36.631 --> 00:59:39.447
And I was like, I'm in the movie business.

1413
00:59:39.447 --> 00:59:41.040
Right.
I'm in the movie business.

1414
00:59:41.040 --> 00:59:41.873
You know what I mean?
(Rich laughs)

1415
00:59:41.873 --> 00:59:43.640
It was so, and I was like,

1416
00:59:43.640 --> 00:59:45.780
I'm on a set, I'm on this whole thing.

1417
00:59:45.780 --> 00:59:48.867
And that was the really wonderful thing.

1418
00:59:48.867 --> 00:59:51.933
And I was happy, man. Like, I'll tell you this.

1419
00:59:53.610 --> 00:59:55.240
I'll never forget being like,

1420
00:59:55.240 --> 00:59:57.360
this is my life now.

1421
00:59:57.360 --> 01:00:01.740
I got settled to this is what it's going to be.

1422
01:00:01.740 --> 01:00:05.530
And I had no idea what was in store for me.

1423
01:00:05.530 --> 01:00:09.800
So you weren't casting your gaze on some goal

1424
01:00:09.800 --> 01:00:13.640
and like working in your free time to move forward on that?

1425
01:00:13.640 --> 01:00:14.520
Well-
You were just like,

1426
01:00:14.520 --> 01:00:16.420
this is where I'm at and this is what it's gonna be?

1427
01:00:16.420 --> 01:00:18.770
But let me tell you, I was working on my free time,

1428
01:00:18.770 --> 01:00:21.940
because what I would do is while I was standing there

1429
01:00:21.940 --> 01:00:22.830
doing that stuff,

1430
01:00:22.830 --> 01:00:26.020
I would write scripts or write down ideas.

1431
01:00:26.020 --> 01:00:28.210
I would go to the library because I couldn't afford

1432
01:00:28.210 --> 01:00:29.043
to buy the books.

1433
01:00:29.043 --> 01:00:32.347
I would buy books on Hollywood and when no one was around,

1434
01:00:32.347 --> 01:00:35.300
'cause sometimes they would have me watching areas

1435
01:00:35.300 --> 01:00:36.350
that they didn't want messed with.

1436
01:00:36.350 --> 01:00:37.720
So there was nobody.

1437
01:00:37.720 --> 01:00:40.140
So I would just read the books all day

1438
01:00:40.140 --> 01:00:41.762
and just stand there and read the books.

1439
01:00:41.762 --> 01:00:42.595
Yeah.

1440
01:00:42.595 --> 01:00:44.300
And just learn about the business

1441
01:00:44.300 --> 01:00:46.050
and learn about all this stuff, and-

1442
01:00:46.050 --> 01:00:48.440
But was it still about being an artist in the business

1443
01:00:48.440 --> 01:00:50.750
or was the idea of being an actor starting to creep in?

1444
01:00:50.750 --> 01:00:51.880
Artist.
Right.

1445
01:00:51.880 --> 01:00:53.020
Artists. Never an actor.

1446
01:00:53.020 --> 01:00:53.880
Never.
But you have had

1447
01:00:53.880 --> 01:00:57.130
that experience on set for "Training Day,"

1448
01:00:57.130 --> 01:00:57.963
right?
Right.

1449
01:00:57.963 --> 01:00:59.689
Which is pretty cool.
But listen,

1450
01:00:59.689 --> 01:01:02.270
this is even freakier.

1451
01:01:02.270 --> 01:01:04.880
I was security on a movie called "End of Days"

1452
01:01:04.880 --> 01:01:05.800
with Arnold Schwarzenegger.

1453
01:01:05.800 --> 01:01:06.800
Uh-huh.

1454
01:01:06.800 --> 01:01:10.030
Jeff Dawn, who is Arnold's makeup artist,

1455
01:01:10.030 --> 01:01:11.650
was walking on the set.

1456
01:01:11.650 --> 01:01:14.470
And he was, he came up to me, and I was a security guard.

1457
01:01:14.470 --> 01:01:16.490
And I was like, okay, extras, go this way.

1458
01:01:16.490 --> 01:01:18.850
You know, crews is over here.

1459
01:01:18.850 --> 01:01:22.660
He looked at me, he said, "Hey man, you ever act?"

1460
01:01:22.660 --> 01:01:25.070
And I went, "Aw, no, sir. That's not me."

1461
01:01:25.070 --> 01:01:29.060
He was like, "Dude, he said, you got a great look, man.

1462
01:01:29.060 --> 01:01:31.790
You need to, I'm serious. You really need to try it."

1463
01:01:31.790 --> 01:01:35.010
He said, "I'm a makeup artist. I do makeup.

1464
01:01:35.010 --> 01:01:36.770
And I'm Arnold's guy.

1465
01:01:36.770 --> 01:01:38.810
I'm here to tell you, you got a great look."

1466
01:01:38.810 --> 01:01:40.270
I thought he was hitting on me.

1467
01:01:40.270 --> 01:01:43.730
So I was like, (laughs) "Uh, thank you, sir.

1468
01:01:43.730 --> 01:01:45.890
Extras are this way and crew is that way.

1469
01:01:45.890 --> 01:01:47.260
Thank you very much."

1470
01:01:47.260 --> 01:01:52.260
Flash forward one year later, I'm an hired actor

1471
01:01:52.770 --> 01:01:56.230
on the movie "The Sixth Day" with Schwarzenegger.

1472
01:01:56.230 --> 01:01:59.992
I go into the makeup trailer. There's Jeff Dawn.

1473
01:01:59.992 --> 01:02:03.500
And he says, "Wait a minute, you look familiar."

1474
01:02:03.500 --> 01:02:06.550
I said, "You told me a year ago.

1475
01:02:06.550 --> 01:02:07.907
that I should be acting."
That's fucking crazy, yeah.

1476
01:02:07.907 --> 01:02:08.740
"And here I am."

1477
01:02:08.740 --> 01:02:11.262
And he said, "Holy shit!"
(Rich laughs)

1478
01:02:11.262 --> 01:02:14.440
Like he said, "I'm making people rich here." (laughs)

1479
01:02:14.440 --> 01:02:16.137
He said, "You're kidding."

1480
01:02:16.137 --> 01:02:19.430
You can't even make this up.
No, that's crazy.

1481
01:02:19.430 --> 01:02:20.263
You can't.

1482
01:02:20.263 --> 01:02:22.080
And you had the security gig that came

1483
01:02:22.080 --> 01:02:24.610
full circle too, right?
Right. Right.

1484
01:02:24.610 --> 01:02:25.733
It's, listen, man.

1485
01:02:25.733 --> 01:02:30.580
When I look at, you know, I remember just every opportunity.

1486
01:02:30.580 --> 01:02:33.050
It's kind of like you realize you,

1487
01:02:33.050 --> 01:02:36.660
if you wrote this stuff, it would be contrived.

1488
01:02:36.660 --> 01:02:37.493
You know what I mean?

1489
01:02:37.493 --> 01:02:40.810
It's truth is stranger fiction in every way.

1490
01:02:40.810 --> 01:02:43.310
And my thing is, you know,

1491
01:02:43.310 --> 01:02:46.570
from being a security guy and acting,

1492
01:02:46.570 --> 01:02:49.690
because we were broke and a friend of mine,

1493
01:02:49.690 --> 01:02:52.570
'cause I was bouncing, I had like two or three jobs.

1494
01:02:52.570 --> 01:02:54.860
I would do the security gig and I would bounce.

1495
01:02:54.860 --> 01:02:55.693
And a friend of mine was like,

1496
01:02:55.693 --> 01:02:58.120
"Hey man, there's this audition for this one show.

1497
01:02:58.120 --> 01:02:59.410
You should try it."

1498
01:02:59.410 --> 01:03:01.653
And I went home and I said, you know, so many,

1499
01:03:01.653 --> 01:03:02.967
I asked my wife, I said,

1500
01:03:02.967 --> 01:03:05.940
"So many people telling me I should try this acting thing."

1501
01:03:05.940 --> 01:03:07.620
She said, "Why not?"
Yeah.

1502
01:03:07.620 --> 01:03:08.820
She said, "Just go."

1503
01:03:08.820 --> 01:03:10.819
And I went and I got it.

1504
01:03:10.819 --> 01:03:11.652
First thing I ever auditioned for.

1505
01:03:11.652 --> 01:03:12.810
Is that the Battle Dome thing?

1506
01:03:12.810 --> 01:03:16.670
Battle Dome, which was American Gladiators on steroids.

1507
01:03:16.670 --> 01:03:17.672
Mm-hmm.
Okay?

1508
01:03:17.672 --> 01:03:19.330
And this is where all the football stuff,

1509
01:03:19.330 --> 01:03:20.960
everything played out.

1510
01:03:20.960 --> 01:03:24.500
And my first time as an actor,

1511
01:03:24.500 --> 01:03:28.050
and now I got the gig and I'm like,

1512
01:03:28.050 --> 01:03:30.786
I don't know anything about what acting is, right?

1513
01:03:30.786 --> 01:03:31.700
(Rich laughs)

1514
01:03:31.700 --> 01:03:33.460
So I did what-
Careful what you ask for.

1515
01:03:33.460 --> 01:03:34.350
Let me tell you what I did.

1516
01:03:34.350 --> 01:03:38.570
I did what I did when the kids would talk about "Star Wars"

1517
01:03:38.570 --> 01:03:40.500
and I would go home and start drawing it out

1518
01:03:40.500 --> 01:03:41.730
and think about what it is,

1519
01:03:41.730 --> 01:03:43.330
even though I hadn't seen the movie,

1520
01:03:43.330 --> 01:03:45.427
what I did was create a character,

1521
01:03:45.427 --> 01:03:48.520
and I said, okay, I don't know how to act,

1522
01:03:48.520 --> 01:03:50.700
but I know what I wanna see.

1523
01:03:50.700 --> 01:03:53.880
So what I'm gonna do is be what I want to see.

1524
01:03:53.880 --> 01:03:56.520
Mm-hmm.
How would I like to be seen?

1525
01:03:56.520 --> 01:03:59.910
Like if I was watching, what would I do?

1526
01:03:59.910 --> 01:04:01.970
And I became the breakout character.

1527
01:04:01.970 --> 01:04:03.770
I became this guy named T-Money

1528
01:04:03.770 --> 01:04:07.310
who was this bad guy from the hood and the whole thing,

1529
01:04:07.310 --> 01:04:10.060
and I was just vicious and mean,

1530
01:04:10.060 --> 01:04:12.360
and I would never let the contestants see me,

1531
01:04:12.360 --> 01:04:13.590
'cause it was a game show

1532
01:04:13.590 --> 01:04:14.480
where we fought.
Yeah.

1533
01:04:14.480 --> 01:04:16.650
They put me in a cage, set the ends on fire

1534
01:04:16.650 --> 01:04:18.570
and rotate the cage, and I would just wrestle

1535
01:04:18.570 --> 01:04:21.530
three contestants in a row, and it was bloody.

1536
01:04:21.530 --> 01:04:24.630
I mean we would leave bleeding every show.

1537
01:04:24.630 --> 01:04:28.130
This metal skin on metal. It was pre-MMA.

1538
01:04:28.130 --> 01:04:30.210
So there was no Ultimate Fighting.

1539
01:04:30.210 --> 01:04:31.530
No one knew anything about that,

1540
01:04:31.530 --> 01:04:33.280
but man, if you look at the clips,

1541
01:04:33.280 --> 01:04:35.287
it was one of the most brutal...

1542
01:04:35.287 --> 01:04:40.260
The log line for the show was "Real Competition, Real Pain."

1543
01:04:40.260 --> 01:04:41.093
Mm-hmm.

1544
01:04:41.093 --> 01:04:42.160
And that's all you need to know. (laughs)

1545
01:04:42.160 --> 01:04:44.533
And you're T-Pain. Yeah.
That's it, right?

1546
01:04:44.533 --> 01:04:47.040
And then, and man, we blasted people.

1547
01:04:47.040 --> 01:04:51.100
But what was happening is I was learning how,

1548
01:04:51.100 --> 01:04:53.040
it was like grand theater.

1549
01:04:53.040 --> 01:04:54.880
It was a lesson. It was a whole thing.

1550
01:04:54.880 --> 01:04:55.820
Like I was...

1551
01:04:55.820 --> 01:04:58.283
And let me tell you, I can't tell you the nerves.

1552
01:04:59.142 --> 01:05:00.680
They would put me behind a wall,

1553
01:05:00.680 --> 01:05:02.890
and every time the wall opened up and the warrior,

1554
01:05:02.890 --> 01:05:04.690
we come out and every time,

1555
01:05:04.690 --> 01:05:06.610
I felt like throwing up every time.

1556
01:05:06.610 --> 01:05:08.300
'Cause I said, I don't know what I'm doing,

1557
01:05:08.300 --> 01:05:09.480
and I wanted to run out of there.

1558
01:05:09.480 --> 01:05:12.020
But I was like, what am I gonna run to?

1559
01:05:12.020 --> 01:05:13.821
What am I gonna run to?

1560
01:05:13.821 --> 01:05:17.850
I'm gonna be back to security? Back to sweeping floors?

1561
01:05:17.850 --> 01:05:20.800
I said, hell no. And I had to go, I had to push.

1562
01:05:20.800 --> 01:05:22.350
It was the thing where

1563
01:05:22.350 --> 01:05:24.620
you've walked halfway across the country,

1564
01:05:24.620 --> 01:05:25.453
what are you gonna do?

1565
01:05:25.453 --> 01:05:26.550
Turn around?
Yeah.

1566
01:05:26.550 --> 01:05:28.480
You know what I mean? Like you gotta keep walking now.

1567
01:05:28.480 --> 01:05:29.990
Yeah. That imposter syndrome.

1568
01:05:29.990 --> 01:05:30.910
I mean, 'cause you don't have,

1569
01:05:30.910 --> 01:05:32.460
you still have never really done

1570
01:05:32.460 --> 01:05:33.707
any kind of formal training, right?

1571
01:05:33.707 --> 01:05:35.807
Mm-mm. Mm-mm.
And you have that story

1572
01:05:37.290 --> 01:05:39.760
of working on "The Expendables" where you have

1573
01:05:39.760 --> 01:05:42.380
that same experience where you have to kind of reckon

1574
01:05:42.380 --> 01:05:44.710
with yourself, like how am I gonna show up for this?

1575
01:05:44.710 --> 01:05:47.280
Yeah. Let me tell you about that.

1576
01:05:47.280 --> 01:05:48.540
What was wild is

1577
01:05:48.540 --> 01:05:52.468
because the sense of entitlement creeped back up.

1578
01:05:52.468 --> 01:05:53.900
I had already done a few movies,

1579
01:05:53.900 --> 01:05:57.780
done some things I end up in "The Expendables"

1580
01:05:57.780 --> 01:06:00.370
and I felt I wasn't getting used enough.

1581
01:06:00.370 --> 01:06:02.290
Like I'm thinking, I'm excited about being

1582
01:06:02.290 --> 01:06:05.740
an action star and I'm sitting in my hotel room,

1583
01:06:05.740 --> 01:06:07.820
and all of a sudden, Sly and everybody,

1584
01:06:07.820 --> 01:06:09.310
they're going on to set and doing.

1585
01:06:09.310 --> 01:06:11.950
Everyone else has done like 30 action movies, right?

1586
01:06:11.950 --> 01:06:12.783
And I haven't even.

1587
01:06:12.783 --> 01:06:16.305
The leading man for like their whole adult lives.

1588
01:06:16.305 --> 01:06:18.263
Dude.
(Rich laughs)

1589
01:06:18.263 --> 01:06:23.263
This is how insidious the attitude of entitlement can be.

1590
01:06:25.370 --> 01:06:28.020
It doesn't matter. It sneaks right in.

1591
01:06:28.020 --> 01:06:29.930
Someone gives you a little bit,

1592
01:06:29.930 --> 01:06:30.763
all of a sudden you're like,

1593
01:06:30.763 --> 01:06:32.818
why didn't I get the whole thing?

1594
01:06:32.818 --> 01:06:36.090
It's so pervasive.

1595
01:06:36.090 --> 01:06:40.060
But I was sitting in my hotel room and I was so mad

1596
01:06:40.060 --> 01:06:42.710
and I would go on and I was like, man,

1597
01:06:42.710 --> 01:06:44.637
instead of just being grateful, I said, you know,

1598
01:06:44.637 --> 01:06:46.750
I'm gonna say my line.

1599
01:06:46.750 --> 01:06:47.583
I'm gonna go home.

1600
01:06:47.583 --> 01:06:49.573
I got an attitude and it just crept in

1601
01:06:49.573 --> 01:06:51.640
and all of a sudden again, man,

1602
01:06:51.640 --> 01:06:53.326
it's that little voice.
Which is insanity,

1603
01:06:53.326 --> 01:06:56.500
given that you've been given this gift to be in this movie.

1604
01:06:56.500 --> 01:06:57.470
Insanity.
Yeah.

1605
01:06:57.470 --> 01:06:59.950
And it's the little voice, the same voice

1606
01:06:59.950 --> 01:07:04.650
that told me about Ken and like, why you mad at him.

1607
01:07:04.650 --> 01:07:08.510
It was like, hey man, you got this opportunity

1608
01:07:08.510 --> 01:07:09.343
of a lifetime.

1609
01:07:10.543 --> 01:07:11.716
What the fuck are you doing?

1610
01:07:11.716 --> 01:07:16.320
Like man, go in there and do your best every time.

1611
01:07:16.320 --> 01:07:17.890
Who cares?

1612
01:07:17.890 --> 01:07:20.630
And so what I did, it hit me.

1613
01:07:20.630 --> 01:07:22.463
I said, "Man, you're right."

1614
01:07:22.463 --> 01:07:24.270
Like all that alone time,

1615
01:07:24.270 --> 01:07:27.690
which I'm very thankful for, really helped

1616
01:07:27.690 --> 01:07:29.040
with the introspection.
Mm-hmm.

1617
01:07:29.040 --> 01:07:30.790
Because what happens is if you got

1618
01:07:30.790 --> 01:07:33.340
too many people around too many crew,

1619
01:07:33.340 --> 01:07:34.810
they can really talk you out of your dream.

1620
01:07:34.810 --> 01:07:35.770
They can really mess you up.

1621
01:07:35.770 --> 01:07:37.890
You like, yeah. You know what you should be.

1622
01:07:37.890 --> 01:07:40.230
Why are you not the start, you know?

1623
01:07:40.230 --> 01:07:45.230
And you get those wrong validating opinions and you're off.

1624
01:07:45.910 --> 01:07:47.950
And that crew will take you right out.

1625
01:07:47.950 --> 01:07:49.530
And I was alone.

1626
01:07:49.530 --> 01:07:50.940
And I went to work the next day

1627
01:07:50.940 --> 01:07:51.960
and I was thankful.

1628
01:07:51.960 --> 01:07:54.880
I said, man, just, I don't care what I'm feeling.

1629
01:07:54.880 --> 01:07:57.420
I said, I'm gonna give everything I got.

1630
01:07:57.420 --> 01:07:59.977
And I walked in. I said hello to everybody.

1631
01:07:59.977 --> 01:08:01.840
And I was like, what's the lines?

1632
01:08:01.840 --> 01:08:03.860
And I started to improv and do,

1633
01:08:03.860 --> 01:08:08.392
and Sly was like, "Man, hey man, I like you, man.

1634
01:08:08.392 --> 01:08:09.703
I'm gonna put you in over here today.

1635
01:08:09.703 --> 01:08:10.970
(Rich laughs)

1636
01:08:10.970 --> 01:08:12.687
You gonna save my life in this movie.

1637
01:08:12.687 --> 01:08:13.973
That's what he told me.

1638
01:08:15.549 --> 01:08:19.430
And I totally changed what happened in that movie.

1639
01:08:19.430 --> 01:08:24.130
That experience changed because of my attitude change.

1640
01:08:24.130 --> 01:08:26.390
Right, so he ends up rewriting the ending

1641
01:08:26.390 --> 01:08:28.810
to make you the hero.
It was, dude.

1642
01:08:28.810 --> 01:08:30.300
And I couldn't believe it.

1643
01:08:30.300 --> 01:08:34.450
And I realized that was so like, that was another lesson.

1644
01:08:34.450 --> 01:08:37.180
Same lesson when I got my scholarship.

1645
01:08:37.180 --> 01:08:40.140
It was that lesson that like, wait a minute, man.

1646
01:08:40.140 --> 01:08:42.930
You know this, you can change this thing.

1647
01:08:42.930 --> 01:08:46.500
You can, your attitude can never, ever waiver.

1648
01:08:46.500 --> 01:08:48.340
You have to have,

1649
01:08:48.340 --> 01:08:50.530
you can't let entitlement creep in

1650
01:08:50.530 --> 01:08:53.010
and steal what's yours.

1651
01:08:53.010 --> 01:08:53.843
Yeah.

1652
01:08:53.843 --> 01:08:56.090
Gratitude being the antidote to in entitlement.

1653
01:08:56.090 --> 01:08:56.923
Exactly. Exactly.

1654
01:08:56.923 --> 01:09:00.280
So how does, I mean, you've spoken a lot about gratitude,

1655
01:09:00.280 --> 01:09:03.230
how you're the most grateful person,

1656
01:09:03.230 --> 01:09:04.900
but gratitude is a practice.

1657
01:09:04.900 --> 01:09:07.300
It didn't come to you easily early.

1658
01:09:07.300 --> 01:09:09.960
So how do you make sure that you're on top

1659
01:09:09.960 --> 01:09:11.410
of your gratitude?

1660
01:09:11.410 --> 01:09:14.020
Well, you know, I basically, you know,

1661
01:09:14.020 --> 01:09:16.770
you look at your results, you look at where you are,

1662
01:09:16.770 --> 01:09:18.620
you look at what's happening, you know what I mean?

1663
01:09:18.620 --> 01:09:22.880
And one thing is I always can, this is one thing,

1664
01:09:22.880 --> 01:09:25.133
even like writing a book, journaling,

1665
01:09:26.159 --> 01:09:30.390
I used to write my top 10 goals down every day.

1666
01:09:30.390 --> 01:09:33.870
And I did that for probably 15 years.

1667
01:09:33.870 --> 01:09:36.237
So, and I had, you know, the Franklin planner.

1668
01:09:36.237 --> 01:09:38.080
You remember those?
Mm-hmm. Yeah.

1669
01:09:38.080 --> 01:09:41.700
I have tons of those stacked up.

1670
01:09:41.700 --> 01:09:43.830
And what helps me with gratitude.

1671
01:09:43.830 --> 01:09:48.560
I go back to 2001, my Franklin planner and look

1672
01:09:48.560 --> 01:09:51.610
at those top 10 goals and I did 'em all.

1673
01:09:51.610 --> 01:09:53.960
Like way exceeded them.

1674
01:09:53.960 --> 01:09:58.397
Like, and then I go back to 2005 and I go, oh, all that.

1675
01:09:58.397 --> 01:10:01.660
And then I go into 2008, I did all that.

1676
01:10:01.660 --> 01:10:05.850
And it's so it fills me with the gratitude

1677
01:10:05.850 --> 01:10:06.780
that I'm supposed to have.

1678
01:10:06.780 --> 01:10:08.577
'Cause you know where you were

1679
01:10:08.577 --> 01:10:11.160
and what was so amazing at that time.

1680
01:10:11.160 --> 01:10:14.453
Like if I could only make a million dollars a year,

1681
01:10:15.420 --> 01:10:16.419
you know what I mean?

1682
01:10:16.419 --> 01:10:21.419
And you go, wow, wow. I've gone way past. (laughs)

1683
01:10:22.215 --> 01:10:24.171
You're like, holy God.
Right.

1684
01:10:24.171 --> 01:10:25.350
It's a weird thing.

1685
01:10:25.350 --> 01:10:26.690
Like I'm really interested in this.

1686
01:10:26.690 --> 01:10:30.710
So, you know, in my own case, like it's wild

1687
01:10:30.710 --> 01:10:31.820
that I'm sitting across from you

1688
01:10:31.820 --> 01:10:33.330
and that I get to do this thing,

1689
01:10:33.330 --> 01:10:36.160
and it's not anything that ever found its way

1690
01:10:36.160 --> 01:10:38.233
into my version of the Franklin planner.

1691
01:10:39.160 --> 01:10:42.840
It's this tension between yes, you wanna set goals

1692
01:10:42.840 --> 01:10:44.450
for yourself and you wanna work hard

1693
01:10:44.450 --> 01:10:46.310
and show up and be diligent.

1694
01:10:46.310 --> 01:10:48.700
But also you have to make space for the miracle,

1695
01:10:48.700 --> 01:10:51.580
because you can't imagine what your life

1696
01:10:51.580 --> 01:10:53.160
could possibly be, right?

1697
01:10:53.160 --> 01:10:54.836
Like you have to, you have to hold

1698
01:10:54.836 --> 01:10:59.170
that tension with the goals that you're seeking

1699
01:10:59.170 --> 01:11:00.390
a little bit loosely.
Yes.

1700
01:11:00.390 --> 01:11:02.960
So you can make space for stuff

1701
01:11:02.960 --> 01:11:04.970
that you can't even predict for yourself.

1702
01:11:04.970 --> 01:11:08.930
And for me, it always goes back to the inside job.

1703
01:11:08.930 --> 01:11:10.610
Like how much am I working on myself?

1704
01:11:10.610 --> 01:11:11.840
Where are my blind spots?

1705
01:11:11.840 --> 01:11:13.410
Where are the things that, you know,

1706
01:11:13.410 --> 01:11:16.290
I could be more grateful or, you know,

1707
01:11:16.290 --> 01:11:18.100
work on some other area.

1708
01:11:18.100 --> 01:11:20.740
And it seems completely disconnected from whatever goal

1709
01:11:20.740 --> 01:11:22.240
I'm seeking in the external world.

1710
01:11:22.240 --> 01:11:25.010
But all I know is that when I'm doing that,

1711
01:11:25.010 --> 01:11:27.150
when I'm engaged in that process,

1712
01:11:27.150 --> 01:11:30.670
that's where I'm sort of taking that insurance policy

1713
01:11:30.670 --> 01:11:34.480
out on greater possibility that I can't quite fathom.

1714
01:11:34.480 --> 01:11:35.313
Exactly.

1715
01:11:35.313 --> 01:11:39.110
And you know, and I had to find these challenges.

1716
01:11:39.110 --> 01:11:41.220
Like there's a thing.

1717
01:11:41.220 --> 01:11:43.760
'Cause you get complacent,

1718
01:11:43.760 --> 01:11:46.090
and it's the comfort zone.

1719
01:11:46.090 --> 01:11:48.600
And there was a time as an actor, you're like,

1720
01:11:48.600 --> 01:11:50.120
you're gonna get hired it.

1721
01:11:50.120 --> 01:11:51.320
It's all good.

1722
01:11:51.320 --> 01:11:54.330
And you're gonna be on a sitcom or some sort show,

1723
01:11:54.330 --> 01:11:55.490
you'll be the fourth lead.

1724
01:11:55.490 --> 01:11:57.570
And you'll be like, and you can just live that life.

1725
01:11:57.570 --> 01:12:01.917
Like, you know, you'll be that guy on the CSI or whatever.

1726
01:12:01.917 --> 01:12:04.238
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine" for the next 20 years.

1727
01:12:04.238 --> 01:12:05.920
And you did it for eight.
Or another version of that.

1728
01:12:05.920 --> 01:12:07.070
But yeah, so.
You can just do.

1729
01:12:07.070 --> 01:12:08.423
But then this is the thing.

1730
01:12:09.280 --> 01:12:14.280
I was like, there's more and I felt too comfortable,

1731
01:12:15.262 --> 01:12:18.380
and it made me weird because I was going,

1732
01:12:18.380 --> 01:12:22.140
I don't like complacency because I'm a little,

1733
01:12:22.140 --> 01:12:24.337
I started to study it and start to look at it.

1734
01:12:24.337 --> 01:12:27.260
And it was like, man, you getting a little,

1735
01:12:27.260 --> 01:12:30.035
like, it's almost like getting chubby.

1736
01:12:30.035 --> 01:12:30.868
(laughs) You know what I mean?

1737
01:12:30.868 --> 01:12:32.810
Where you're like, I'm just eating the same things

1738
01:12:32.810 --> 01:12:35.210
and I'm not really, I said, I need a challenge.

1739
01:12:35.210 --> 01:12:36.910
I need something that's gonna excite me.

1740
01:12:36.910 --> 01:12:41.080
So I started, I forced myself into hosting.

1741
01:12:41.080 --> 01:12:41.913
Forced myself.
Mm-hmm.

1742
01:12:41.913 --> 01:12:46.810
Like you gotta understand, 240-pound muscular,

1743
01:12:46.810 --> 01:12:49.380
big guy was not the first guy

1744
01:12:49.380 --> 01:12:51.241
you would think of as a television host.

1745
01:12:51.241 --> 01:12:53.200
Yeah, but you got the charm and the charisma.

1746
01:12:53.200 --> 01:12:54.951
But again-
And the smile and the teeth

1747
01:12:54.951 --> 01:12:56.250
and the whole thing.
Well, now.

1748
01:12:56.250 --> 01:12:58.750
Yeah. (laughs)
At the time,

1749
01:12:58.750 --> 01:13:01.350
people were like, does he?
Mm.

1750
01:13:01.350 --> 01:13:03.880
I know him from "Everybody Hates Chris."

1751
01:13:03.880 --> 01:13:07.270
I know him from, you know, this kind of thing.

1752
01:13:07.270 --> 01:13:11.280
And so I remember going in and doing an audition

1753
01:13:11.280 --> 01:13:13.080
for "Who Wants to Be a Millionaire,"

1754
01:13:13.080 --> 01:13:14.717
the daily version of that.

1755
01:13:14.717 --> 01:13:16.580
And let me tell you, I could tell you that,

1756
01:13:16.580 --> 01:13:19.110
it was actually a failure, okay?

1757
01:13:19.110 --> 01:13:23.700
That was not a good look because here I was

1758
01:13:23.700 --> 01:13:24.900
on a game show,

1759
01:13:24.900 --> 01:13:28.300
the daily version of a show that had been on TV for years.

1760
01:13:28.300 --> 01:13:31.240
And I'm this character who talked way too loud

1761
01:13:31.240 --> 01:13:32.240
for the daytime audience.

1762
01:13:32.240 --> 01:13:34.270
Hey, how you guys doing today? Yeah!

1763
01:13:34.270 --> 01:13:37.180
So I overcompensated, and it was a little much

1764
01:13:37.180 --> 01:13:38.687
for that audience 'cause they, you know,

1765
01:13:38.687 --> 01:13:41.130
the people were like, "Well I'm drinking my tea

1766
01:13:41.130 --> 01:13:42.120
and he's screaming at me.

1767
01:13:42.120 --> 01:13:42.953
Stop screaming."

1768
01:13:42.953 --> 01:13:44.770
All the notes were like "stop screaming," you know?

1769
01:13:44.770 --> 01:13:46.150
Uh-huh.

1770
01:13:46.150 --> 01:13:48.280
And here I was and I would do this daily thing.

1771
01:13:48.280 --> 01:13:53.150
I did 360 episodes in a year

1772
01:13:53.150 --> 01:13:56.050
of "Who Wants to Be a Millionaire"

1773
01:13:56.050 --> 01:13:58.482
while I was doing "Brooklyn Nine-Nine."

1774
01:13:58.482 --> 01:13:59.361
Ah, you were doing it

1775
01:13:59.361 --> 01:14:00.194
at the same time, wow.
So I would fly

1776
01:14:00.194 --> 01:14:03.290
to the east coast, do the show, and then do like,

1777
01:14:03.290 --> 01:14:05.340
we would do almost seven episodes in a day.

1778
01:14:05.340 --> 01:14:06.900
Like, bap-bap-bap-bap-bap-bap all day.

1779
01:14:06.900 --> 01:14:08.460
I was exhausted, then fly back

1780
01:14:08.460 --> 01:14:10.896
and go right to "Brooklyn Nine-Nine."

1781
01:14:10.896 --> 01:14:13.160
But I said, I have to get this.

1782
01:14:13.160 --> 01:14:17.100
Like it was hook or cook. I said, I'm going to learn this.

1783
01:14:17.100 --> 01:14:18.690
And it gave me an opportunity to do it.

1784
01:14:18.690 --> 01:14:20.650
And let me tell you, it didn't go well,

1785
01:14:20.650 --> 01:14:22.970
because they fired me the first year.

1786
01:14:22.970 --> 01:14:24.170
I mean, when the year was over,

1787
01:14:24.170 --> 01:14:27.320
they were like we're gonna, they went with Chris Harrison.

1788
01:14:27.320 --> 01:14:28.336
I had to understand.

1789
01:14:28.336 --> 01:14:30.190
I was like, "Oh man, it just didn't work."

1790
01:14:30.190 --> 01:14:32.870
So there I was, I said, I gave, I put my heart.

1791
01:14:32.870 --> 01:14:35.720
I had actors that are like, "Why are you doing this?"

1792
01:14:35.720 --> 01:14:37.067
They would come up to me and they would say,

1793
01:14:37.067 --> 01:14:39.660
"Terry like, man, you could be doing this acting.

1794
01:14:39.660 --> 01:14:40.890
Why don't you do some more movies?

1795
01:14:40.890 --> 01:14:41.723
And why don't you do this?"

1796
01:14:41.723 --> 01:14:43.930
I said, because what I saw was the future.

1797
01:14:43.930 --> 01:14:47.550
I saw, I got tired of waiting to be picked.

1798
01:14:47.550 --> 01:14:48.383
Mm-hmm.

1799
01:14:48.383 --> 01:14:50.680
And I said, if I learn this skill,

1800
01:14:50.680 --> 01:14:52.300
then I don't have to be picked.

1801
01:14:52.300 --> 01:14:54.580
I could choose. I can choose.

1802
01:14:54.580 --> 01:14:56.030
You know, there's not a lot of people.

1803
01:14:56.030 --> 01:14:58.880
Like when you look at big super stars or people,

1804
01:14:58.880 --> 01:15:00.810
just charm doesn't mean you could host.

1805
01:15:00.810 --> 01:15:01.650
It doesn't mean you could do

1806
01:15:01.650 --> 01:15:02.584
all that stuff.
Yeah.

1807
01:15:02.584 --> 01:15:03.992
'Cause it's a whole nother thing.

1808
01:15:03.992 --> 01:15:05.110
I said, I have to learn this.

1809
01:15:05.110 --> 01:15:07.210
And man, what was so wild,

1810
01:15:07.210 --> 01:15:09.420
and to this day, this is when you,

1811
01:15:09.420 --> 01:15:11.893
this is to your point about leaving room.

1812
01:15:12.740 --> 01:15:15.660
I'm hosting the number one talent show in the world.

1813
01:15:15.660 --> 01:15:17.742
Yeah.
The fourth year.

1814
01:15:17.742 --> 01:15:21.850
And I look at this, like this was never on the list.

1815
01:15:21.850 --> 01:15:24.160
I didn't know it would go this far.

1816
01:15:24.160 --> 01:15:26.880
I was just trying to secure a future,

1817
01:15:26.880 --> 01:15:28.990
hopefully do some things.

1818
01:15:28.990 --> 01:15:31.710
And you know, that would allow me to pivot

1819
01:15:31.710 --> 01:15:34.470
in different ways, but never did I see this.

1820
01:15:34.470 --> 01:15:35.440
Yeah it's crazy.

1821
01:15:35.440 --> 01:15:36.885
It's crazy.
It's crazy. It's crazy.

1822
01:15:36.885 --> 01:15:38.148
It's the biggest show in the world.

1823
01:15:38.148 --> 01:15:40.510
And I gotta tell you, I think, you know,

1824
01:15:40.510 --> 01:15:42.840
I will admit to being somewhat dismissive

1825
01:15:42.840 --> 01:15:44.840
of the whole host thing, like, ah, come on.

1826
01:15:44.840 --> 01:15:47.140
That's not like real acting.

1827
01:15:47.140 --> 01:15:50.110
That's not, you know, they're reading off the prompter

1828
01:15:50.110 --> 01:15:52.960
and the whole thing, but I just had to host

1829
01:15:52.960 --> 01:15:54.460
an all-day conference in Miami,

1830
01:15:54.460 --> 01:15:55.617
I do a fair amount of speaking.

1831
01:15:55.617 --> 01:15:56.450
(Terry laughs)

1832
01:15:56.450 --> 01:15:57.670
I was like, how hard could it be, you know?

1833
01:15:57.670 --> 01:16:00.330
And I go there and I'm like, holy shit.

1834
01:16:00.330 --> 01:16:02.620
Like I'm responsible for holding the energy

1835
01:16:02.620 --> 01:16:04.090
for this group of, you know,

1836
01:16:04.090 --> 01:16:05.640
this huge auditorium of people

1837
01:16:05.640 --> 01:16:07.420
for like an eight hour stretch.

1838
01:16:07.420 --> 01:16:08.947
Like I better get my shit together.

1839
01:16:08.947 --> 01:16:09.780
Yep.

1840
01:16:09.780 --> 01:16:11.710
Ended up like rehearsing for eight hours,

1841
01:16:11.710 --> 01:16:15.840
wrote a 35-page script and, you know,

1842
01:16:15.840 --> 01:16:18.890
had to go out in between each speaker and come up

1843
01:16:18.890 --> 01:16:19.930
with jokes on the fly

1844
01:16:19.930 --> 01:16:20.940
and figure out.
Yes.

1845
01:16:20.940 --> 01:16:22.710
And I ended up leaving that experience,

1846
01:16:22.710 --> 01:16:25.820
A, with just a huge amount of respect for how difficult

1847
01:16:25.820 --> 01:16:28.390
that job is and how specific that skillset is

1848
01:16:28.390 --> 01:16:31.470
and how different it is from typical public speaking,

1849
01:16:31.470 --> 01:16:34.220
and also any other kind of like performance-oriented thing

1850
01:16:34.220 --> 01:16:35.860
that I'd ever done,

1851
01:16:35.860 --> 01:16:38.480
but also it's completely thrilling.

1852
01:16:38.480 --> 01:16:40.010
Like it's so gratifying.

1853
01:16:40.010 --> 01:16:41.850
Like 'cause you are like,

1854
01:16:41.850 --> 01:16:44.183
you have to have these people in your hands,

1855
01:16:44.183 --> 01:16:45.016
you know?
That's right.

1856
01:16:45.016 --> 01:16:46.660
And if, when you're in your kind of flow

1857
01:16:46.660 --> 01:16:49.340
and you know that you're firing on all cylinders,

1858
01:16:49.340 --> 01:16:50.380
it's a pretty cool thing.

1859
01:16:50.380 --> 01:16:52.150
Let me tell you, you know what I like to call it,

1860
01:16:52.150 --> 01:16:54.263
they give me the keys to NBC.

1861
01:16:55.100 --> 01:16:56.150
Yeah.
I get the keys.

1862
01:16:56.150 --> 01:16:57.350
I mean, it's the biggest-
You have it.

1863
01:16:57.350 --> 01:16:59.240
It's the biggest show in the world.

1864
01:16:59.240 --> 01:17:01.450
Wait, and it goes ding ding ding

1865
01:17:01.450 --> 01:17:06.450
and you have NBC in your hand, there's only you.

1866
01:17:08.300 --> 01:17:10.680
And everyone's looking at you

1867
01:17:10.680 --> 01:17:13.600
and it's like those doors open, you walk out,

1868
01:17:13.600 --> 01:17:16.110
and you are representing everything.

1869
01:17:16.110 --> 01:17:17.750
You're representing 30 Rock.

1870
01:17:17.750 --> 01:17:19.100
You're representing Simon Cowell,

1871
01:17:19.100 --> 01:17:20.320
you're representing every act.

1872
01:17:20.320 --> 01:17:21.530
You're representing what the show means.

1873
01:17:21.530 --> 01:17:23.730
It's been on for 17 years,

1874
01:17:23.730 --> 01:17:26.890
and you have it in your hands.

1875
01:17:26.890 --> 01:17:31.620
And it's like, Aw! And it reminded me of the NFL.

1876
01:17:31.620 --> 01:17:33.530
You know what I mean? It was that thrilling.

1877
01:17:33.530 --> 01:17:35.070
Like the ball could go anywhere, boo.

1878
01:17:35.070 --> 01:17:37.540
Oh, and it's live by the way. Live.

1879
01:17:37.540 --> 01:17:39.330
So anything can happen.
Right.

1880
01:17:39.330 --> 01:17:41.750
You know, I'll never forget, the prompter goes out.

1881
01:17:41.750 --> 01:17:43.150
It happens.

1882
01:17:43.150 --> 01:17:45.807
You still have to keep the show going

1883
01:17:45.807 --> 01:17:47.502
and you have to explain the rules and other,

1884
01:17:47.502 --> 01:17:48.437
because there's rules.

1885
01:17:48.437 --> 01:17:49.910
There's things that people want to try to win money.

1886
01:17:49.910 --> 01:17:53.600
You can't miss, you misinterpret it, people get sued.

1887
01:17:53.600 --> 01:17:55.872
It's crazy. It's that pressure.

1888
01:17:55.872 --> 01:17:56.917
And then the prompter goes out

1889
01:17:56.917 --> 01:17:59.360
and you still have to know what you're talking about,

1890
01:17:59.360 --> 01:18:01.270
and it's like, oh my God.

1891
01:18:01.270 --> 01:18:03.320
And dude, I go home. I'm exhausted.

1892
01:18:03.320 --> 01:18:06.610
Every fiber am I being is exhausted.

1893
01:18:06.610 --> 01:18:10.050
And I'm happy, like I just played in the Super Bowl.

1894
01:18:10.050 --> 01:18:11.160
Yeah.
You know what I mean?

1895
01:18:11.160 --> 01:18:12.170
Yeah, 'Cause you're the glue.

1896
01:18:12.170 --> 01:18:15.710
I mean the panelists have to weigh in on the acts.

1897
01:18:15.710 --> 01:18:16.543
Exactly.

1898
01:18:16.543 --> 01:18:18.480
But they're not responsible for keeping the show moving.

1899
01:18:18.480 --> 01:18:19.706
Not at all.
Yeah.

1900
01:18:19.706 --> 01:18:22.040
I go to them for an answer.

1901
01:18:22.040 --> 01:18:24.860
I give them a question, but then I'm responsible

1902
01:18:24.860 --> 01:18:26.403
for bringing the act out, introducing these guys,

1903
01:18:26.403 --> 01:18:28.370
explaining the rules to the public.

1904
01:18:28.370 --> 01:18:30.600
I am, and it's so thrilling.

1905
01:18:30.600 --> 01:18:32.837
I can't, like you said, do it,

1906
01:18:32.837 --> 01:18:34.980
and then you think it's full,

1907
01:18:34.980 --> 01:18:37.229
the place is full of, you know,

1908
01:18:37.229 --> 01:18:40.380
probably 6,000 people in the (indistinct).

1909
01:18:40.380 --> 01:18:41.213
Mm-hmm.

1910
01:18:41.213 --> 01:18:42.600
And then you turn around and say, no,

1911
01:18:42.600 --> 01:18:45.140
it's really a hundred million people watching.

1912
01:18:45.140 --> 01:18:46.750
That kind of thing.
Yeah that's a mind fuck.

1913
01:18:46.750 --> 01:18:49.140
Holy yeah. (laughs)

1914
01:18:49.140 --> 01:18:51.890
We had Oz Pearlman in here the other day,

1915
01:18:51.890 --> 01:18:53.330
you remember him, the Mentalist.

1916
01:18:53.330 --> 01:18:54.163
Yes. Whoops.
(ice clatters)

1917
01:18:54.163 --> 01:18:57.482
Look at me, I'm sorry. I'm making' all this noise.

1918
01:18:57.482 --> 01:19:00.340
I think he ended up third maybe five years ago

1919
01:19:00.340 --> 01:19:01.634
or something like that.

1920
01:19:01.634 --> 01:19:02.467
Do you remember that guy?

1921
01:19:02.467 --> 01:19:04.290
Yes. Yes.
He was the Mentalist.

1922
01:19:04.290 --> 01:19:05.850
Yes. The Mentalist, yes.

1923
01:19:06.704 --> 01:19:07.537
I remember him.
He's pretty cool.

1924
01:19:07.537 --> 01:19:08.780
He was screwing with our heads.

1925
01:19:08.780 --> 01:19:09.613
Dude, we get some great-
I know.

1926
01:19:09.613 --> 01:19:11.980
It's unbelievable the quality of talent

1927
01:19:11.980 --> 01:19:14.025
and all the stuff that you get to see.

1928
01:19:14.025 --> 01:19:17.200
But I'm gonna tell you this and let me tell you,

1929
01:19:17.200 --> 01:19:21.010
every act that comes through reminds me of me.

1930
01:19:21.010 --> 01:19:24.950
It reminds me of being in Flint, Michigan,

1931
01:19:24.950 --> 01:19:28.270
dreaming of being some and somehow involved

1932
01:19:28.270 --> 01:19:31.130
in entertainment, you know, no matter what it is

1933
01:19:31.130 --> 01:19:34.210
and everybody had you huge, huge obstacles

1934
01:19:34.210 --> 01:19:35.669
to get to that point.
Mm-hmm.

1935
01:19:35.669 --> 01:19:36.740
Huge.

1936
01:19:36.740 --> 01:19:38.880
And a lot of people say, well, you know, yeah,

1937
01:19:38.880 --> 01:19:40.510
who hates the sob story and the whole thing,

1938
01:19:40.510 --> 01:19:43.290
but there's not an entertainer that doesn't,

1939
01:19:43.290 --> 01:19:45.140
'cause it's all rejection.
Yeah.

1940
01:19:45.140 --> 01:19:45.973
All of it.

1941
01:19:45.973 --> 01:19:49.540
You just have to find ways to deal with your rejection.

1942
01:19:49.540 --> 01:19:53.610
And so I look at these acts and I'm their a bodyguard.

1943
01:19:53.610 --> 01:19:55.000
I'm their protector.

1944
01:19:55.000 --> 01:19:58.490
I'm the person that gives them

1945
01:19:58.490 --> 01:20:00.357
what they need on the way out,

1946
01:20:00.357 --> 01:20:02.966
and then I'm the person that counsels them

1947
01:20:02.966 --> 01:20:06.170
when they come back out from the audience,

1948
01:20:06.170 --> 01:20:07.003
you know what I mean?

1949
01:20:07.003 --> 01:20:09.300
And it's like, if it doesn't go well,

1950
01:20:09.300 --> 01:20:11.428
I let them know that this is just the beginning.

1951
01:20:11.428 --> 01:20:13.310
Mm.
And if it goes great,

1952
01:20:13.310 --> 01:20:16.600
I let them know you gotta come back next week with more.

1953
01:20:16.600 --> 01:20:19.859
You know, it's just, and it reminds me of me.

1954
01:20:19.859 --> 01:20:23.530
And also I try to the people that are successful,

1955
01:20:23.530 --> 01:20:26.370
I really, really try to pull something

1956
01:20:27.210 --> 01:20:28.870
and learn something from them.

1957
01:20:28.870 --> 01:20:29.703
Yeah.
You know what I mean?

1958
01:20:29.703 --> 01:20:32.200
And it's just a beautiful relation.

1959
01:20:32.200 --> 01:20:33.417
I learn a lot doing that show, man.

1960
01:20:33.417 --> 01:20:34.950
That's cool.
'Cause you meet

1961
01:20:34.950 --> 01:20:38.140
so many different people and it's one of the highlights

1962
01:20:38.140 --> 01:20:41.480
of my life, but it really, really was

1963
01:20:41.480 --> 01:20:44.365
one of those things that I had to get

1964
01:20:44.365 --> 01:20:46.160
out of my comfort zone.

1965
01:20:46.160 --> 01:20:49.773
I had to find something new and I'll be honest with you,

1966
01:20:49.773 --> 01:20:51.500
I don't know what's next.

1967
01:20:51.500 --> 01:20:52.960
I've been designing furniture.

1968
01:20:52.960 --> 01:20:53.793
Right.

1969
01:20:54.710 --> 01:20:56.097
And my wife is like, "Why are you doing this?"

1970
01:20:56.097 --> 01:20:58.093
I'm like, I don't know.

1971
01:20:59.130 --> 01:20:59.963
I need a challenge.
You gotta feed

1972
01:20:59.963 --> 01:21:01.670
the artist though, right?
You have to.

1973
01:21:01.670 --> 01:21:02.800
Are you painting still?

1974
01:21:02.800 --> 01:21:05.510
I'm painting, I'm drawing, I'm sketching.

1975
01:21:05.510 --> 01:21:07.520
Still designing I'm in the fourth iteration

1976
01:21:07.520 --> 01:21:10.710
of my collection with Bernhardt,

1977
01:21:10.710 --> 01:21:12.160
but there's something else coming.

1978
01:21:12.160 --> 01:21:16.520
Like right now I've been challenging myself to do, you know,

1979
01:21:16.520 --> 01:21:18.470
more dramatic things.

1980
01:21:18.470 --> 01:21:20.977
You know, I did a stint on a show called

1981
01:21:20.977 --> 01:21:24.653
"Tales of the Walking Dead," which is not funny at all.

1982
01:21:25.690 --> 01:21:26.960
I love that challenge.

1983
01:21:26.960 --> 01:21:30.690
I see myself doing, but this is another thing, man,

1984
01:21:30.690 --> 01:21:34.620
writing these books and being so vulnerable and putting

1985
01:21:34.620 --> 01:21:37.700
my story out there is like moving from fiction

1986
01:21:37.700 --> 01:21:40.150
to nonfiction, is what I like to say.

1987
01:21:40.150 --> 01:21:43.210
It's literally the next step,

1988
01:21:43.210 --> 01:21:47.610
what I feel in my career is it is beyond being an actor,

1989
01:21:47.610 --> 01:21:51.290
but being a person of interests, you know?

1990
01:21:51.290 --> 01:21:55.370
Where let's talk about things together, being, you know,

1991
01:21:55.370 --> 01:21:58.470
'cause this is another thing like being a host,

1992
01:21:58.470 --> 01:22:02.010
it's almost like being able to have conversations

1993
01:22:02.010 --> 01:22:05.950
with people and learn from people and tell your story

1994
01:22:05.950 --> 01:22:09.020
is the only way we're gonna get by problems.

1995
01:22:09.020 --> 01:22:12.150
This is why you and I are sitting here right now.

1996
01:22:12.150 --> 01:22:14.920
It's because this is nuance.

1997
01:22:14.920 --> 01:22:17.950
And what we are witnessing right now is the death of nuance.

1998
01:22:17.950 --> 01:22:20.280
Mm-hmm.
No one is talking.

1999
01:22:20.280 --> 01:22:22.410
Everyone is screaming at each other.

2000
01:22:22.410 --> 01:22:25.220
Just look at any daytime talk show.

2001
01:22:25.220 --> 01:22:28.130
It's all yelling at each other.

2002
01:22:28.130 --> 01:22:31.980
And at the end of the day, it's like, you know,

2003
01:22:31.980 --> 01:22:34.361
we showed you, pow, you know, mic drop.

2004
01:22:34.361 --> 01:22:36.160
Yeah.
And we'll see you tomorrow.

2005
01:22:36.160 --> 01:22:38.190
And I'm going...
It gets the hits.

2006
01:22:38.190 --> 01:22:39.660
It charges the dopamine,

2007
01:22:39.660 --> 01:22:42.870
but ultimately everybody as a human being

2008
01:22:42.870 --> 01:22:47.870
has that deep desire for connection and for nuance.

2009
01:22:48.970 --> 01:22:52.640
And I think that's a big reason why, you know, podcasts

2010
01:22:52.640 --> 01:22:54.015
have grown in popularity.

2011
01:22:54.015 --> 01:22:55.850
People would tell me all the time,

2012
01:22:55.850 --> 01:22:58.150
like you gotta make these things 30 minutes.

2013
01:22:58.150 --> 01:23:01.140
Like who's gonna listen to you guys banter on forever?

2014
01:23:01.140 --> 01:23:03.410
But you know, it's been quite the opposite,

2015
01:23:03.410 --> 01:23:05.010
because I think people are starving for it.

2016
01:23:05.010 --> 01:23:07.190
Starving.
Honestly, it is, you know,

2017
01:23:07.190 --> 01:23:10.890
our version of the campfire and we need that campfire

2018
01:23:10.890 --> 01:23:13.890
if we wanna solve our problems and the solution

2019
01:23:13.890 --> 01:23:17.340
to our problems lies in appreciation of nuance.

